const OPI_QA=[{"title":"一、日常话题 WARM UP & WIND DOWN","subs":[{"title":"Flight","qa":[{"q":"What type of a/c do you fly?","a":""},{"q":"Do you like this type of a/c?","a":""},{"q":"Which airline are you working for?","a":""},{"q":"Do you like your company logo?","a":""},{"q":"Do you think you are lucky to be a pilot?","a":"15, Can you describe your first solo?"},{"q":"How do you like your flight instructor?/ What do you think of your flight","a":"instructor?"},{"q":"Can you give a brief introduction of your flight school?","a":"18, Do you prefer to fly international routes or domestic routes?"},{"q":"Do you prefer to long routes or short routes?","a":""}]}]},{"title":"二、复杂气象","subs":[{"title":"颠簸TURBULENCE","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever encountered/experienced/suffered turbulence during flight?","a":"When? Where? Yes + story (as a pilot orapassenger): When I flew from xx to xx last year. I encountered (light/moderate/severe)turbulence. The a/c was shaking badly (and I tried to control it) some passengers got injured/no one was injured after a moment everything went back to normal at last we safely landed. No, I never encountered a turbulence but I know it's dangerous to flight..."},{"q":"Why is turbulence hazardous/dangerous to flight safety?","a":"PILOT:hardto control, maneuver A/c: lose speed and altitude, structural damages PASSENGERS&CREW:safety.injuries/fatalities Firstly for pilot it's hard to control the a/c in turbulence; Secondly,the a/c maylosespeed and altitude,even loss of control, and suffer structural damages in severeturbulence; Third, crew and passengers might be injured in turbulence. 3. Besides turbulence,what otherbad/adverse weatherconditions doyouknow? Thunderstorm, sandstorm, heavy rain, hail, icing, volcanic ashes, lightning strike, CBs..."},{"q":"Why is CAT (clear air turbulence) extremely dangerous? What threat does CAT pose","a":"to flight safety? CAT is clear air turbulence and usually occurs at high level with a CAvOK condition, and cannot be detected/predicted, it would cause a bumpy flight with shuddering and shaking before pilot can issue a warning. severely it will cause structural damages and onboard injuries,"},{"q":"Do you have any suggestions on reducing risks of incidents caused by dangerous","a":"weather? How can the pilots avoid turbulence/CAT or bad weather? Prefight: collect information from any sources before theflight for preparation, such as NOTAM,ATIS,ATC,METARreports ifnecessary:delay/changetakeoff RwY simulator training, accumulate more flying experience. Inflight:monitorweather radar,monitorPiREP/ATC reports,visual check the sky ofany significant weather change, and be aware of the areas with large temperature gaps. if necessary:go round/deviate/circumnavigate/detour, level change. Consider flying at a different time of day: The air is typically more stable in the morning compared to the afternoon or night. Thermals haven't built up yet and winds are usually not as strong early in the morning. BUCKLE UP: Always make sure that ypu and all other passengers are securely strapped in. This will help prevent injuries if you encounter turbulence. Maintain control of the aircraft: Don't accelerate or try to fight past the bumps. Maintain control while flying through turbulence and remain calm. Before my flight I will make a good preflight preparation, and collect weather information from ATIS, ATC, NOTAM and preceding aircraft. (check weathers reports on my takeoff airport/en-route and destination airport), if there's a bad weather overhead airport or RWY, I will request to delay or change my takeoff RWY. During flight I will monitor weather radar and collect weather reports, have good visual check, if there's a bad weather ahead I will request to change level or deviate to avoid it. If adverse weather conditions exit over the destination airport, I will divert or hold until it improves 6. When there's passenger severely/seriously/badly injured/bleeding during turbulence, if you were the captain of an aircraft, what would you do? a. CAPTAIN: b. CREW: C. PASSENGERS: d. ATC: e. AIRPORT: Firstly, I will focus on my control, remain stable flight, and let crew to take care of the passengers and give him the first aid.if possible, find a doctor on board. Secondly, inform ATC my situation, request priority landing and ambulance on arrival."},{"q":"Whatiswaketurbulence?","a":"Wake turbulence is a bumpy/chop air created by preceding aircraft (generated from the wings/airframe and other parts, it goes outward and upward then sinks below, and it lasts longer in calm wind conditions, the bigger the aircraft, the stronger wake turbulence it produces), wake turbulence happens when climbing, taking off and landing. it endangers flight safety of following aircraft, we should keep a good separation when departing and arrivingbehindotheraircraft."},{"q":"Is wake turbulence dangerous to the flight?","a":"Of course, especially the wake turbulence generated by wing tip vortices, if the preceding aircraft is heavy and close, it will cause the following aircraft dificult to control or even out of control, lose altitude and heading, reduce aircraft performance, cause structural damages, also cause possible passenger&crew injuries or even crash."},{"q":"When would you expect to encounter wake turbulence?","a":"If the preceding aircraft is close and big, we are very likely to encounter wake turbulence, if the departing and arriving separation is poorly allocated, we will encounter wake turbulence, wake turbulence also can be generated by a climbing traffic and exit on difference levels, cause influence to traffic flying through these altitudes."},{"q":"How do you avoid wake turbulence?","a":"ATC should keep standard separation for all departing and arriving aircraft, pilot should strictly follow the separation rules and monitor frequency carefully, . If a heavy aircraft is just taking off or landing ahead, the pilot behind must wait at least 3 minutes. If a medium aircraft is ahead, the pilot behind must wait no less than 2 minutes. Preceding Aircraft Following Aircraft Minimum Separation SUPER HEAVY 5.0 NM SUPER MEDIUM 7.0 NM SUPER LIGHT 8.0 NM HEAVY HEAVY 4.0 NM HEAVY MEDIUM 5.0 NM HEAVY LIGHT 6.0 NM MEDIUM 5.0 NM Minimum time separation for arriving aircraft not radar-separated is: 2minutesfor a MEDIUM aircraft behind a HEAVY aircraft a HEAVYaircraft behinda SUPER aircraft 3minutes for a LIGHT aircraft behind a HEAVY or MEDIUM aircraft aMEDIUMaircraftbehinda SUPERaircraft 4minutes for a LIGHTaircraft behind a SUPER aircraft Minimum time separation for departing aircraft which are using: thesamerunwayor parallel runways separated byless than760m(2500ft)or crossing runways if the projected flight path of the second aircraft will cross the projected flight path of the first aircraft at the same altitude or less than 300 m (1 00oft)belowor parallel runways separated by 760 m (2 500 ft) or more, if the projected flight path of the second aircraft will cross the projected flight path of the first aircraft at the same altitude or less than 300 m (1 000 ft) below. is2minutesbetween a HEAVY aircraft taking off behind a SUPER aircraft a LIGHT or MEDIUM aircraft taking off behind a HEAVY aircraft a LIGHT aircraft taking off behind a MEDIUM aircraft and 3 minutes between a LIGHT or MEDIUM aircraft taking off behind a SUPER aircraft."}]},{"title":"积雨云、雷、雷暴","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever encountered/experienced lightning strike during the flight?","a":"Yes, I encountered lightning strike when I flew from Xx to Xx, I was a passenger and 1 saw a flash heard a big bang, luckily the a/c didn't suffer any structural damage and we safely landed at last. No, but I heard of it from my friends, he suffered lightning strike when he flew... No,I never encountered it but I know it's dangerous to flight. In what kind of weather conditions does lightning strike /thunderstorm/CB most likely to occur? As far as I know,it's most likely to occur in summer,in thunderstorm and CBs when there are strong convective currents in the air. Lightning is visible electrical discharge from a cloud, this happens when there is an imbalance of charges between a region of the cloud and another surfaces. Lightning is usually associated with cumulonimbus clouds (thunderclouds), but it also occurs in stratiform clouds (layered clouds with a large horizontal extent), in snowstorms and dust storms, and sometimes in the dust and gases emitted by erupting volcanoes. During a thunderstorm, lightning can occur within the cloud, between clouds, between the cloud and the air, or between the cloud and the ground. The moving air within the cloud builds up electric charges as it slides past other air. The buildup of electric charges allows lightning to form, much in the same way that create a strikes. It often happens after you see the bolt of lightning because sound travels more slowly than light."},{"q":"Why is lightning dangerous/hazardous to flight safety? What damages may","a":"lightning strike cause to the aircraft? 外部 - structural damage(configuration/depressurization/fuel tank explosion). 内部 - system failure(electronic equipment, electrical fire, smoke/communication/na vigation system, instrument short circuit). 此外雷电会模糊飞行员视线，造成短暂失控temporaryblindness 可能危机机内乘员安全 Lightning strike may cause structural damages to a/c and (penetrate into a/c) systems failure. For example, it can damage configuration/control surface of a/c and even cause explosion to fuel tank(this will endanger/threaten flight safety). Besides, it will damage navigation and communication systems The last incident when a lightning strike ended in a catastrophe occurred in 1963.The lightning caused a crash of a Pan American Boeing 707 in Maryland, the U.S. and 81 passengers died on board. Investigators concluded that lightning-induced ignition of the fuel/air mixture in a fuel tank was at fault. The crew lost control of the plane after the outer left wing disintegrated due to the explosion."},{"q":"If you suffered lightning strike/ if your aircraft was struck by lightning in flight,","a":"what would you do? PILOT: have a good control, follow SOP, execute checklist, identify damage: ATC: report ATC of my situation request diversion if serious: AIRPORT: maintenance on arrival. I will keep my a/c under control, follow SOP and execute checklist, and identify(check) the damage,(程度) if my a/c suffered any major damage, I will report to ATC and request diversion to alternate airport and ask for ground maintenance inspection. (例)For example,ifthelightning strike caused navigationfailure,Iwill reporttoAC (use raw data, backup instrument)and request radar vectors to my destination/alternate airport.(And Iwill also request maintenance onarrival)"},{"q":"What can pilots do to avoid lightning strike? How can pilots reduce the danger of","a":"encountering lightning strike? Before flight, I will check weather reports on NOTAM and get weather update(PIREP) from ATC, monitor ATIS.(if there's any lightning overhead the airport or my takeoff runway. I will request for delay or change my takeoff runway): During flight, I will monitor my weather radar (precisely), if there's any indication on my radar, I will request to deviate/offset to get clear of it."},{"q":"How does lightning be created?","a":"Lightning is created in cloud, if there is a strong convective current and friction in the flow of air, the lightning is likely to be produced.(如对流天气强烈的积雨云或者浓积云 CB or TCU) 相关图片描述"},{"q":"What are the stages/phases of thunderstorm?","a":"Itincludes3stages: a. Developing stage: full of updrafts and moisture in the air condenses into water droplets as the warm air rises, storm clouds gradually form. Mature stage: tiny water droplets grow larger and continuous droplets accumulating in the cloud makes it look dark and gray, the rising air could not hold the droplet and become to raindrop/rainfall and pulling a downward cool air called downdraft and storm clouds are fully formed, in a thunderstorm cell there is a cycling of updraft. downdraft and rain, and the moving air in cloud builds up electric charges and forms lightning雷and thunder响雷 Dying/Dissipating stage: downdraft becomes stronger than the updraft, and warm moist air can no longer rise and cloud droplets no longer forms, the storm dies out with light rain as the cloud disappears from bottom to top. The whole process takes about one hour for an ordinary thunderstorm. Severe thunderstorms like supercells and squall lines are much larger, more powerful, and last for several hours."},{"q":"How do pilots go round the thunderstorm of CB?","a":"描述绕飞过程 Firstly, we will have an indication of CB on radar (have CB insight/be informed of CB by ATC) and then we report to ATC and request deviation, we can either offset right or left until we get clear of the weather."},{"q":"How do you search for CBs during flight?","a":"During flight, I use weather radar to search for CB, if there's CB, it shows in red on radar. Ialsouse visual check/observeto scan the sky. Besides, I can get information of CB from other pilot report or ATC report."},{"q":"What should you do if there is a thunderstorm (at the airport)?","a":"During flight: I will request to hold until the weather gets better or request to divert at alternate airport or go round it."},{"q":"What damages might CBs bring to your aircraft? Why CBs are dangerous?","a":"As far as I know, if we fly into CB, We will encounter multiple bad weathers such as heavy rain, hail, lightning strike, strong wind turbulence, icing and so on. Pilot: will feel hard to control a/c or even impossible to recover from CB; A/c: will suffer major/severe/critical structural damages and system malfunction; CREW/PAX:will sufferinjuries/casualties, even deaths/fatalities."},{"q":"How do you make the weather analysis before each flight?","a":"Before flight, we can get weather information from dispatching office, ATis or ATC. During preflight briefing, we will discuss weather information in the briefing room."},{"q":"If you encounter heavy rain during approach, what factors will you consider?","a":"目视偏高，落地不精确，扎实接地 I will consider the visibility风挡遮挡, if it is beyond limit I will go around; overrun/veer off the runway due poor braking action."}]},{"title":"风切变WINDSHEAR","qa":[{"q":"What is wind shear? Where is it likely to occur?","a":"Wind shear is the sudden/rapid change in wind speed/velocity and direction, wind shear canoccuranywhereinthesky. When the wind abruptly changes direction or speed over a small distance, then this scenario is referred to as wind shear, ICAO defines two major wind shear - horizontal wind shearandvertical wind shear."},{"q":"Do you have wind shear warning in your cockpit? Have you triggered the wind","a":"shearwarning? No, I am a student pilot and I fly DA-40/CESSNA 172, we receive wind shear information from pilot report and weatherreport; But I know EGPWS system has reactive wind shear function, it calculates AOA, bank angle, altitude and airspeed and other data into FAC (Flight Augmentation Computer), whenever these data exceed limitation, it triggers a warning to pilot to indicate a he's suffering wind shear. For example A320 B737 if we suffered wind shear, we can hear \"wind shear, wind shear\" And aircraft also have predictive wind shear system from onboard aircraft radar (predictive function depends on doppler radar to detect moisture in the aircraft for a wind shear prediction, in 1980s onboard radar started to detect the frequency shift and microwave pulses cause by the microburst), it will predict wind shear ahead and send \"wind shear ahead\" warning messages to pilots before get caught by wind shear. And many large airports now have TDWRs (Terminal Doppler Weather Radar) installed near the airport to help detect changes in wind speed and direction. Something we should know about the development of wind shear predication Early systems relied on aircraft sensors to determine the presence of wind shear. These systems were of limited value as they were unable to predict the onset of wind shear. Later systems use doppler weather radar, or other systems such as laser or infra-red technology 红外技术 to predict wind shear. Many modern aircraft, such as the B777 Series, have Predictive Wind Shear (PWS) warning systems which collect wind velocity data gathered by the weather radar to identify the existence of wind shear. These systems have a short range, and are dependent on the radar picking up velocity data from water and ice particles ahead of the aircraft and, consequently. don't work in dry conditions. However, they are effective, providing the pilot with an opportunity to abort the take-off or carry out a missed approach."},{"q":"If the wind shear warning is ignored by pilots what are the consequences?","a":"What effects might wind shear bring to your aircraft performance? Pilot will definitely fly into wind shear if he ignored a wind shear warning, the suffer the consequencea wind shearbrings. PILOT: hard to control and lots of corrective action to align the wind or wind shear recovery procedure which leads to a high pilot workload: A/c: instrument indication of a altitude loss/height loss, lose or increase speed or aerodynamic stall,more severely damage a/c; CREW&PASSENGERS:onboard injuries. It will cause aircraft to chop up and down, bumpy, lose airspeed, altitude, increase or decrease aircraft performance and cause aircraft difficult to control. And pilot will suddenly in. a high workload stress to try to recover the a/c."},{"q":"Why is low level wind shear especially dangerous to flight safety?","a":"PILOT: not enough time and altitude to respond and recover from the wind shear: A/C: sudden loss of airspeed and altitude, stall in short time, severe damages or even crash; CREW&PASSENGERS: injuries and even deaths. Because the LLws happens all of a sudden in low altitude (usually with 200Oft of the surface), during takeoff, initial climb, approach or landing phase of flight, pilot may not have time to perform wind shear recovery procedure/make response/take actions due to low altitude. Low level wind shear causes A/C to lose speed and altitude, and stall (in short time), it will make A/C loss of control, or even crash and bring injuries and even deaths to crews and passengers. Typical Scenarios An aircraft on initial climb encounters a microburst with strong down-drafts, which prevent the aircraft from climbing away, even though the pilot immediately recognizes the wind shear and takes correct action. An aircraft on approach in tail-wind conditions encounters horizontal wind shear resulting in a change of wind component to a head-wind (increasing performance shear); the IAs increases and the aircraft climbs above the glideslope. The pilot attempts to salvage the landing and the aircraft touches down late and fast and overshoots the runway."},{"q":"Do you have any opinion on reducing accidents caused by wind shear? What are","a":"your suggestions on how to reduce accidents caused by wind shear? Preflight: weather forecast, ATIS, NOTAM, wind check, if necessary, request holding/delaytakeoff,changeRWY; During flight: weather radar, PIREP, ATC and pilot reports, f necessary, request deviate; Upon landing: go around Before my flight, I will collect weather information from ATIS, read NOTAM and check with PIREP (recent weather reports and forecasts). Checking for visual observation, assess weather with crew experience on airport environment and prevailing wind condition. If any wind shear reported overhead the airport,I will delay takeoff or change takeoff RWY until it's clear; If we still decideto flight under windy condition: Select the most favorable runway, considering the location of the likely wind shear/downburstcondition; Select the minimum flaps configuration compatible with takeoff requirements, to maximize climb-gradient capability: Use the weather radar (or the predictive wind shear system, if available) before beginning the takeoff to ensure that the flight path is clear of hazards; Select maximum takeoff thrust: After selecting the takeoff/go-around (TOGA) mode, select the flight-path-vector display for the monitoring pilot (PM/PNF), as available, to obtain a visual reference of the climb flight path angle; and, Closely monitor the airspeed and airspeed trend during the takeoff roll to detect any evidence of impending wind shear.\" If wind shear occurs during approach, I will go around. There's stil some precautions need to be done in post flight phase to prevent windshear Improved forecasting of wind shear: Improved training in wind shear recognition, avoidance and recovery: More widespread use of ground and airborne wind shear warning systems."},{"q":"If you fly into wind shear, how can you recover from it?","a":"Perform/carry out/execute wind shear recovery procedures immediately, use full power/push the throttle to maximum, pitch up to 15 degrees, maintain configuration. at the same time, pay attention to airspeed, altitude and attitude. Wind Shear Recovery The Briefing Note advises that \"if wind shear is encountered during the takeoff roll or during initial climb, the following actions should be taken without delay: Before V1: The takeoff should be rejected if unacceptable airspeed variations occur (not exceeding the target V1) and if there is sufficient runway remaining to stop the airplane; AfterV1: Disconnect the auto throttles (A/THR), if available, and maintain or set the throttleleverstomaximumtakeoff thrust; Rotatenormallyat Vr;and, FollowtheFDpitch command if theFD provides wind shearrecovery guidance,or set the required pitch attitude (as recommended in the aircraft operating manual (AOM)/quickreferencehandbook(QRH): During initial climb: Disconnect the A/THR, if available,and maintain or set the throttle levers to maximumtakeoffthrust; If the autopilot (AP) is engaged and if the FD provides wind shear recovery guidance, keep the AP engaged; or, Follow the FD pitch command, if the FD provides wind shear recovery guidance; or, Set the required pitch attitude (as recommended in the AOM/QRH); Level the wings to maximize the climb gradient, unless a turn is required for obstacle clearance; Closely monitor the airspeed, airspeed trend and flight-path angle (as available); Allow airspeed to decrease to stick shaker onset (intermittent stick shaker activation) while monitoring the airspeed trend; Do not change the flaps or landing-gear configurations until out of the wind shear condition; and, When out of the wind shear condition, increase airspeed when a positive climb is confirmed, retract the landing gear, flaps and slats, then establish a normal climb profile.\" During initial climb: Select the takeoff/go-around (Take-off / Go-around (TO/GA) Mode) mode and set and maintain maximum go-around thrust Follow the Flight Director pitch command (if the FD provides wind shear recovery guidance) or set the pitch-attitude target recommended in the AOM/QRH If the AP is engaged and if the FD provides wind shear recovery guidance, keep the AP engaged: otherwise, disconnect the AP and set and maintain the recommended pitch attitude Do not change the flap configuration or landing-gear configuration until out of thewind shear Level the wings to maximize climb gradient, unless a turn is required for obstacle clearance Allow airspeed to decrease to stick-shaker onset (intermittent stick-shaker activation)while monitoring airspeed trend Closely monitor airspeed, airspeed trend and flight path angle (if flight-path vector is available and displayed for the PNF) and, increase the airspeed when a positive climb is confirmed and establish a normal climb profile,"},{"q":"What do you know about micro-burst? what dangers may micro-burst bring to","a":"aircraft? Micro-burst is a localized extreme weather and contains variable wind shear conditions within (headwind, downdraft and tailwind within the area in a short time), it creates a column of fast sinking air through the base of a storm cloud, which spreads horizontally upon hitting the surface, cause great threats to landing and takeoff aircraft. Microbursts are short-lived, usually lasting from about 5 to 15 minutes, and they are relatively compact, usually affecting an area of 1 to 3 km (about 0.5 to 2 miles) in diameter a. When approaching- strong headwind, increase indicated airspeed and increase lift, airplane will pitch up and increase performance; b. When flying thru- strong down draft and lose altitude, even crash; C. When leaving - strong tailwind, reduce airspeed and lift, even stall. 相关图片描述 下滑道 8.1 When approaching to the rotation speed wind shear is encountered and airspeed decreases suddenly, what could you do? Because our speed is above V1, we must continue takeoff immediately/without hesitation/decisively during wind shear, we should monitor airspeed, altitude and attitude carefully when lift off for precaution, use the maximum throttle to make sure we airborne successfully. 详见上面V1后，风切变改出程序"}]},{"title":"积冰ICEACCUMULATION","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever encountered icing condition during flight?","a":"Yes, I encountered icing when I flew from BJ to SH, I saw ice buildup/accumulation on wings, (the captain/l) executed anti-icing function, (I reported ATC) and we changed level. At last, we landed at destination airport safely. No, but 1 heard one accident happened in 2020, 1 former military instructor and 2 student pilots suffered icing when carrying out cross country training from Yichang to Enshi in Hubei Province, it was a DA42 and their aircraft suddenly accumulated severe ice before they can react, and then the aircraft lost significant lift and stalled, and crashed to a mountain somewhere in Changyang. The icing accident caused loss of lives of all 3 crews onboard,RiP."},{"q":"In what kind of condition willicing occur? And in what area does icing most likely","a":"to occur? 条件：air temperature is below O degrees,aircraft surface temperature is below degrees,enoughsupercooleddroplets过冷水滴inair 区 域 : icing usually occur in CB or thunderstorm area, icing will accumulate on windshield, leading edge of wings, air intake of engine, pitot tube and so on. icing is easy to occur in winter if the temperature is low and humidity is high (especially in CB), and also it usually accumulates on front of wings, windshield."},{"q":"Are there any icing indications in your cockpit? How do you know you are in icing","a":"conditions ? How can a pilot in the cockpit know he is experiencing icing conditions inflight? I fly DA 40, there is no instrument and indication for icing. During flight, we get icing reports from ATC and pilot, we also visually check wings and windshield for any possible ice accumulation, also if the temperature is below O, and the icing condition by monitoring airspeed and altitude. Before flight, especially in winter operation, we make walk around inspection on skin, control surfaces, pitot tube to find out ice accumulation. detection."},{"q":"How can icing conditions affect the flight safety? Why is icing dangerous to A/c?","a":"Change shape of wings, reduce lift(to maximum 30%) and aerodynamic performance. increase drag(to maximum 40%) and weight: b. Jam flight control surfaces, make aircraft hard to control; If ingested to ENG, reduce power, cause ENG flame out: d. Block pitot tube, static port cause instrument unreliable, indication of airspeed and altitudemaybeincorrectand inaccurate. e. Cause disturbance of CG and cause aircraft unstable inflight ice onwindshield will block visual sight 9. ice on radio antenna will interrupt communication h. ice on gear well and undercarriage will affect the retraction and extension 5. If ice built up on the wings what would you do? AIRPORT:de-ice beforetakeoff; A/c: pitot heating function, cabin heating, cabin ventilation, apply more thrust, back bleed air,emergency window-open if necessary. ATC: report and request level change or deviate the icing area. I fly DA-40, before flight, usually we make walk around check and remove ice from wings, spray de-ice fluid, during flight, we can level change, turn around to avoid icing, and open pitot heating function, increase thrust to avoid stall. 飞入结冰区 尽快离开结冰区 改变高度战调头等 空速管加温 座舱加温 座舱通风 推力手榜 普大功率 备用气源 打开 皮急窗 按需打开 如空速管加温失效： 备用静压源 打开 应急前 关闭 飞机积冰会明显增加飞机失速速度 What do you know about the anti-ice system in an aircraft? As far as I know, anti-ice system uses the electrical power or engine hot bleed air to heat up the surfaces that are most likely to suffer icing on aircraft by evaporating the water attachment and melting up ice, surfaces including but not limited to windshield, pitot tube, engine air intake, leading edge of wings and etc. In DA4O we use hot bleed air to de-ice. 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"大侧风STRONGCROSSWIND","qa":[{"q":"If you notice the crosswind exceeds the limitation, but your captain insists on","a":"landing, what will you do? 超过限制必须复飞，任一机组成员(Captain,FO,Observer)喊出复飞口令，机组成员必 须执行。 According to the company operation manual, If the crosswind exceeds the company limitation, we must go around. But if my captain still insists on landing, I will have a good communication with him, tell him the reason.I will always follow SOP. Finally, the captain has the final say/decision/authority."},{"q":"If the preceding aircraft goes around due to crosswind, what will you do?","a":"Wind changes very fast, we must decide according to the wind limitations (speed and direction) If it exceeds the limitation we must go around, if not we can continue approach."},{"q":"If crosswind is close to your standard, what will you do? if crosswind is marginal","a":"around your standard, what will you do? Before flight: change takeoff runway, request delay for a moment: b. During rolling:prepare for possible takeoff rejection/abortion if wind is marginal; C. Before landing:we should prepare for possible go around if wind is around minimum, monitor airspeed and altitude precisely.try to use appropriate technique to adjustfrom windycondition 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"顺风 TAILWIND","qa":[]},{"title":"低能见LOWVISIBILITY","qa":[{"q":"What's the policy in low visibility situatiohs? What's your company's policy for","a":"departing and landing in low visibility conditions? During my flight training if the visibility is below minimum(1600m),we will suspend the training and hold on ground until the visibility is improved. In civil airports, a conduction of LVO (Low Visibility Operation) is effective if under low visibility condition (usuallybelowRVR400),"},{"q":"How can you avoid runway incursion in low visibility? How to avoid ground","a":"collision in case of lowvisibility? Monitor frequency, listen and readback ATC clearance carefully, confirm if we have any doubts on incursion. Taxi with slow speed, turn on allights especially anti-collision lights, and make sure we enter the correct runway. Request follow me car if necessary and always have a good communication and task sharing in cockpit, maintain good situation awareness."},{"q":"After landing at an unfamiliar airport, if you miss the first rapid exit, what will you","a":"iop Normally this situation won't happen because we will learn airport chart and take trainings on simulator before we fly, if it happens I will stop aircraft and report ATC to vacate from next exit. Stop the aircraft immediately, report to ATC, request vacate the runway at the next exit: b. If there is no next rapid exit or the next rapid exit is far, I will request backtrack from present position."},{"q":"In heavy fog, if you miss the correct taxiway what will you do?","a":"Normally this situation won't happen because heavy fog taxing is strictly forbidden by CAAC, but if I missed the correct taxiway in heavy fog, I will: a. Pilot: stop the a/c immediately: b. A/C: turn all my lights on (including anti-collision lights): C. ATC: report to ATC, mark position and standby further instructions;: d. Airport: request follow-me car if necessary. 相关图片描述"}]}]},{"title":"三、非正常情况","subs":[{"title":"延误DELAY","qa":[{"q":"Have you encountered delays more than one hour?","a":"Yes, last year when I flew to Singapore Changi Airport, the captain was told to hold en route because of weather, finally we arrived at destination airport with one hour delay. Yes, last month when I was training in Yichang airport, we had to hold on ground due to lots of departing and arriving aircraft, so we had a 2 hours of training delay."},{"q":"What might be the reasons for delays? What situations can cause delay?","a":"Airport: adverse weather, flow control, military activities; runway maintenance, poor landing conditions; medical or fire assistance/emergency evacuation on runway, de-icing, refueling, loading cargo（ground service）...（机场运营，环境因素，飞机服务，特情..） PILoT: not familiar with chart/airport/procedures, poor flying sklls, poor communicationwithATC. PASSENGERS: loading process and security process. A/c: emergency aircraft, departing and arriving separation allocation. I think there are many reasons for delay. The main reasons(common) reasons are: bad weather, flow control, military activities, andalsoground services intheairport. (Besides) Delay is also caused by unexpected situation from pilot, passengers and status(conditions) of a/c."},{"q":"Do you think large airport may have more delays?","a":"航空器多，VIP航班多，客流量大，安检、装卸货压力大，滑行道长,ATC工作强度大 A/c (main reason): lots of departing and arriving a/c AIRPORT: long taxiway, congested airspace ATC: deal with many a/c, heavy workload PASSENGERS: baggage handling, security check Yes, I think so, large airports have more delays because: Big airports have more a/c and airspace is congested, the traffic is busier and there are a. moreViPflights, sometimesflowcontrol is executed. b. And large airports have to handle massive/many passenger baggages and security checks, A/c usually spendslots oftime on taxiing. d. Besides.ATC are always under high workload. Do you have any ideas for reducing delays? /How do you think we can reduce delays?/Do you have any suggestions to reduce delay? Inmyview/opinion,therearemanywaystoreducedelays: ATC/ground staff should improve work efficiency (to reduce time on handling a/c, passengersandbaggage); Airport should improve management and construction to handle more aircraft (equipment,technology): Pilot should improve flying skills by simulator training and accumulate more flying hours; Good communication between ATC and pilot is also necessary to help reduce delays 参考答案2 ATC&AIRPORT: optimize flight planned route, improve cooperation of company dispatch, air crew and ATC. Timely update weather report and other report related to airport. If possible, straight-in approach. Increase boarding efficiency, update new approach and navigation methods like RNAV, CDFA. Pilot: good communication with ATC, request possible route that saves time, skillful enough to handle all unexpected situation during flight."},{"q":"What do you think of airlines' compensating policy on passengers after long","a":"delays? Delay caused by force majeure/natural disasters (covid-19 pandemic) should not be compensated; Delay caused by airlines management/operation should be compensated by any means: cash, one night accommodation, send passengers to other connecting flights. On the one hand, if the delay is caused by airlines such as mechanical problems, aircraft dispatch, poor management and so on, I think it is necessary to compensate. On the other hand, if the delay is caused by uncontrollable situations such as bad weather, military activities, It is then unnecessary for airlines to compensate passengers."},{"q":"If your flight has been delayed for a long time, the passengers became anxious and","a":"unhappy, what would you do? Comforting passenger is normally a cabin crew's job, there are some approaches to stop the complaint when delayed, offering food and drinks; keep passengers well informed with timely updates, patiently explain to them what happened, what caused the delay and time of delay, improve cabin services."}]},{"title":"改航备降DIVERSION","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever experienced diversion?","a":"Yes, last year when I flew from xx to xx, one passenger is incapacitated in flight, the captain made a PA to inform us of diversion, and we landed at an alternate airport."},{"q":"What will cause an aircraft to divert? What are the reasons for diversion","a":"A/C: system failure (ENG, control surfaces), structural damages, emergency situations (fire, depressurization), low endurance; Passenger&crew:bomb, terrorists, hijacker. sick/injured passengers. crew incapacitation; Destination Airport: bad weather over destination, military activities, low visibility..."},{"q":"Do you have any alternate airport planned for each flight? what you will consider","a":"when choosing alternate airport? According to CAAC regulation, alternate airports must be planned for each instrument flight/IFR for safety concerns. I will consider fuel endurance, altitude, emergency services, runway condition, weather and some other factors when choosing an alternate airport. During my cross-country training we have alternative airport planned en-route."},{"q":"If you have to divert, what information of the alternate airport is necessary for","a":"pilots to know in advance? If you want to divert, what information do you need to get about your alternate airport? If I have to divert, I will get information about the alternate airport such as weather, RwY in use/available, RwY conditions, ATiS, missed approach procedure, type of approach, airport facilities and emergency assistance/services and so on. How can ATc help if you want to make a diversion? What kinds of support do you usually need from ATc if you want to make a diversion? a. ATC can make enough separation for our aircraft to avoid conflicting traffic, and give us clearance to divert; b. ATC can vector us to the alternate airport: C. ATC can provide necessary information of weather and alternate airport; d. ATC can contact the alternate airport emergency services for us. Job of ATC separation of theaircraftfrom othertraffic providing diversion clearance or facilitating delaying tactics by providing vectors or a holdingclearance g. prioritization of the emergency aircraft for landing allowing fora long final if requested h. assigning a discreteradiofrequency whenpossible advising the airport emergency services and all concerned parties in accordance with localprocedures/protocols providing any information that might be requested bythe crew such as weather,type of approaches available,runway length and other pertinent aerodrome details"},{"q":"What do you need to do before your diversion? What preparations are needed","a":"before you divert to your alternate airport? First, I will make the decision and inform flight crew, check the fuel/endurance, choose the best alternate airport. Second, I will report to ATC and advise my purpose/intention, and request clearance and guidance to the alternate airport. And get information of alternate airport Last, I will make a PA to tellthe situation to the passengers and crew, let them be ready for diversion. Contact dispatcher/company of my diversion."}]},{"title":"炸弹威胁BOMBSITUATION","qa":[{"q":"If you received an anonymous call saying an explosive item on board, what would","a":"you do? Pilot: stay calm, have good control of a/c and follow SOP, perform checklist (declare emergency,7700...); Crew:letcrewidentify/search/findbomborsuspiciousbags Passengers: PA for emergency evac in advance (no need to tell bomb situation to passengers for unnecessary panic): ATC:report and request priority landing/diversion/returning; Airport:request remoteapron for landing,airport assistance (fire/medical/bomb squad) on landing,executeemergencyevacuationon landing/arrival. 亦可分飞行阶段：来源于机组SOP If my a/c is on the ground, I will immediately evacuate/deplane/disembark the passengers, and let my crew to find suspected/suspicious item/bomb.and report to ATC and requestfireengines/trucks and bombdisposal squad. If mya/c is in the air,I will declare emergency,ask the purser/cabin crewto check the bomb, report to ATC and request landing at the nearest airport. I will also request fire/medical services and bomb squad on arrival/on landing/in attendance. After landing, execute emergency evacuation immediately. 2. How to prevent bomb threat from happening? a. Airport and airlines should apply more advanced security equipment and devices. b. Execute more training to ground staff. C. Airport security management should have a better cooperation with police and fire departments. d. Increase public security awareness of passengers and staff."}]},{"title":"结构损伤STRUCTURALDAMAGE","qa":[{"q":"What may cause aircraft structural damage?","a":"Bird strike, FOD(Foreign Object Damage). lightning strike, explosion, severe turbulence ,"},{"q":"If your aircraft suffers from structural damage, what will you do?","a":"During flight, If our aircraft suffers from structural damage, we will identify the damage and see if a diversion is required, check aircraft status and flight condition, then report our situation to ATC, necessarily we will declare emergency. After landing, we will note on logbook and report the structural damage to mechanics formaintenanceinspection."}]},{"title":"外物损伤和外物吸入FOD&FOI","qa":[{"q":"In which phases of flight is an aircraft most likely to suffer bird strike?","a":"Bird strikes often occur during takeoff or landing. final approach or initial climb, or during low altitude flight in the daytime.(AGL500 feet).(Because bird activities usually occur at low level andduring daytime). 出现鸟类活动的因素 Habitat features, including open areas of grass and water as well as shrubs and trees, provide food and roosting sites for birds.Even transient water accumulation on uneven pavements can be a significant bird attractant. Landfill and other waste disposal sites often attract large numbers of birds if they are not carefully managed. Some types of agricultural activity, on or in the vicinity of an airport, may attract birds. Migrating birds often follow well-defined flight paths in considerable numbers. This can create a hazard if the flight paths are near an airport. Airports in coastal locations often have a much higher level of un-managed bird activity than do inland airports. Most airports contain considerable areas of grass within their perimeters. Even dry grass can be attractive as a loitering area for birds by day or night."},{"q":"How does bird strike endanger flight safety? What might be the consequences of","a":"bird strike? AsfarasIknow,bird strikemaycause: a. It may crack/break/penetrate the windshield (cause cockpit crew injuries): b. It will cause structural damages to wings, fuselage, landing gear and control surfaces, make a/c hard to maneuver/control or even lose control, cause gear extension problems thrust, If ingested to ENG, causeENGlostpower and flame C. out/shutdown/fire/vibration(surge)/disintegration. d. Relatively rare but a bird strike at high level will cause damages to sealing structure and leadtoadepressurization Typical Scenarios e. Bird ingestion to three out of four engines of a departing jet transport occurs at 200 feet agl after take off has been made despite ATC advice of the presence of large birds and an offer to have them dispersed. As a result, one engine is disabled completely and two others are sufficiently damaged to the extent of only producing reduced thrust. An emergency return to land is made. f. A flock of medium-sized birds is struck by a jet transport just after V1 but before Vr with a rejected take off response despite take off performance being limiting due to aircraft weight, As a result, an overrun occurs with substantial aircraft damage. A twin-engined light aircraft flies into a single heron at 200 feet agl after take off and it breaks through the windscreen and hits the pilot who temporarily loses control so that upon recovery,aforced landing ahead is the only option h. Wing root damage to a single-engined light aircraft caused by a vulture-strike during climb out causes structural damage to such an extent that control is lost and terrain impactresults."},{"q":"What can be done to avoid bird strike? What measures can be taken to prevent","a":"bird strike? Preflight: monitor ATIs, check ATC and pilot reports before takeoff, if necessary, change takeoff runway or request holding for delay: During flight and landing: check pilot reports and have a good visual of sky, if necessary,goaround; PILOT: collect bird information (ATIS, ATC, other pilots) before takeoff and landing, have a good visual scan of sky; AIRPORT: collect and analyze bird data, apply anti-bird equipment (dispersing equipment). Habitat management, including reduction or elimination of trees, shrubs and other plants which provide food, shelter or roosting sites for birds. Netting or draining of streams, routinely wet grassland and areas of standing water. Prevention of transient formation of such areas after heavy rainfall. Aerodrome grass management appropriate to the prevalent species and the degree of risk that they pose. Grass height maintenance can be very important. Liaison with local authorities to ensure that landfill waste disposal sites are not operated so as to create an aircraft hazard. Liaison with local farmers to limit the attraction of birds to fields. Use of bird scaring techniques such as: Broadcast of bird distress signals; Firing of pyrotechnic bird scaring cartridges What would you do if you encounter bird strike? a. During flight - prepare for possible go around, if able, continue landing. b. Before flight - holding until get clear of birds. 相关图片描述"},{"q":"If the aircraft engine ingests foreign object, will it endanger flight safety?","a":"Yes,inmyopinion: If ENG ingests foreign object, it will damage blades (engine fan, cowling), cause ENG flame out, fire, surge or vibration and failure. The ENG power and performance will be reduced andthreaten flight safety. Especially during takeoff, it will cause critical damage to ENG because ENG is at maximum power at that time."},{"q":"Which phases(stages) of flight is engine ingestion most likely to occur? Why?","a":"Engine ingestion is most likely to occur during ground operation, takeoff and landing. During taxing and rolling, engine is close to ground and easy to ingest foreign object like rubber, plastic bags and debris on ground. During takeoff and landing, bird is likely to be ingested to ENG."},{"q":"If your aircraft engine ingests some foreign object during takeoff rolling , what","a":"iop nok pinom Before V1: Abort takeoff immediately, apply abortion procedure to stop the airplane and evacuation. After V1: Continue takeoff to airborne, join holding pattern, follow SOP perform checklist, check my a/c condition and try to maintain the altitude, if the status is good, I will continue flight. If condition is bad, request returning, after landing, contact ground staff/mechanics to check my a/c. 其他航司教员答案 According to the regulation, if any bird is ingested to ENG we must return for landing, at the same time we will report immediately to ATC, stop our climb, execute checklist and SOP, inform passengers and crew the situation and possible evacuation on landing, after landing. requestmaintenance inspection. What can be done to prevent FOD from happening? Pilot should visual check the ground and sky, and inform ATC of the debris on the ground. Delay takeoff or change runway if necessary, more simulator training. Airport must keep the taxiways and runways clean, and disperse birds away. Passenger must improve safety awareness and stop tossing/throwing objects when boarding. Good communication with ground staff. 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"应急撤离EMERGENCYEVACUATION","qa":[{"q":"Does your company have emergency evacuation training?","a":"every2years inmycompany. 根据公司的规定，每两年我们和乘务员进行一次紧急撤离培训。这种训练对于紧急撤离 的安全实施非常重要。通过训练，机组和乘务员将熟悉标准紧急撤离程序，他们会清楚撤离 过程中的重要事项。这样，在有必要进行紧急撤离时，可以采取一切预防措施。 According to the regulation, we have such training every two years and we train together with other crew members to get familiar with the procedure of evacuation."},{"q":"Under what circumstance does a pilot need to carry out emergency evacuation?","a":"When it is necessary to execute emergency evac? 737-800口决：机火油电炸 当出现下列情况：地面出现发动机火警警告，地面出现APU火警警告，飞机冲(偏)出跑道 部分起落架着陆，或地面滑跑时起落架折断，飞机结构严重损坏，客舱内火警、烟雾且无法 控制，报告飞机上有炸弹，或其他紧急情况对旅客和机组有生命威胁时，机组应考虑实施撤 离。 应急撤离决策 应急撤离有可能造成旅客的伤亡，机长在决策时应慎重。 出现下列情况机长必须实施应急撤离： (1)机体明显破损; (2）机上烟雾火灾无法控制 (3）燃油严重泄漏； (4)飞机迫降； (5）机上有明确爆炸物; (6）机长认为需要执行应急撤离的其他情况。 Major and significant structural damage, uncontrollable fire and smoke Serious fuel leak, force landing,identified bomb/explosives (and any situation that threatens flight safety needs to evacuate by captain's decision)"},{"q":"How do you prepare the evacuation? What will you do to execute an evacuation?","a":"What should the flight crew do when they decide to perform emergency evacuation? 应急撤离分为有准备撤离和无准备撤离，在突然的特情发生时，无准备撤离更加危险。机长 根据紧急情况的严重程度及机组将采取的行动计划，通知ATC并申请紧急援助； a. ATC:Contact/reportATC of intention requestemergency assistance. 尽早通知乘务组以便他们有充足的时间做好撤离的准备工作； b.Crew members: inform and let them preparefor emergency evacuation. 机长调配最佳机组力量，复习着陆操纵动作和撤离程序： Captain: review landing maneuver and evacuation procedure (checklist/consider landing position). 发布旅客广播通知撤离： d. PA to inform passengers of evacuation. As far as I know, we should follow SOP: Captain: make decision and inform cabin crew, review evacuation maneuver and procedure; Passengers&Crew: Make a PA to inform passengers&crew for evacuation after landing, also advise crew to be prepared for the evacuation; ATC&Airport: report to ATC of the intention and evacuation, request ground assistance. 航线飞行员答案 First, we will report our intention to ATC that we will execute emergency evacuation. Second, we will make a PA to inform cabin crew and passengers that we will make emergency evacuation. Third, we will complete the emergency evacuation checklist. Fourth, the cabin crew will open the emergency exits and emergency slides and lead the passengers to leave the aircraft in time and in order. We will be the last one to leave the aircraft. 应急撤离的程序 一旦飞机停住、刹住停留刹车后，机长通知客舱机组，要求 ECAM 动作。 在这一阶段， 机组的任务分工如下： 副驾驶执行ECAM动作 机长根据情况做出撤离的决定。应考虑以下因素： 释放灭火剂后仍然不能控制火情 可能在跑道上让旅客撤离飞机 将意图或请求告知 ATC 如果在释放灭火剂后仍然不能控制火情，机长要求应急撤离程序 4. How fast should you execute the emergency evacuation? According to the regulation, when emergency evacuation is executed, all crews and passengers should evacuate out of plane within 90 seconds."},{"q":"Do you think a good briefing with the cabin crew may improve the chances of a","a":"successful evacuation?Why? It will improve cooperation and efficiency of evacuation, captain and crew will be familiar with each others work and respond quickly when emergency happens."},{"q":"How many evacuation slides are there on your a/c? What are the locations of these","a":"slide? I fly DA-40, we don't have slides. But as far as I know, there are 4 slides on B737 and A320. When we execute emergency evacuation, the slides will be deployed/released to help passengers andcrew deplane/evacuate/disembark quickly."},{"q":"How do you feel during times of emergencies? Are you very stressed? Have you","a":"everpanic? I didn't experience emergencies during my flight but I know an experienced pilot should calm down, follow sOP and perform checklist, ensure flight safety of all crews and passengers."},{"q":"Do you think cabin crew play an important role in emergency evacuation?","a":"Cabin crew can help comfort passengers and maintain cabin order during emergency evacuation; Monitor cabin and report abnormal situations. Chances of a successful evacuation depends on the training, responding and cooperationofcabincrew."},{"q":"Who would be the last one to leave the aircraft in the event of emergency","a":"evacuation? According to the operation regulation, the captain should be the last one to leave the aircraft. Because captain is the commander of the aircraft and he/she should be responsible for the safety of passengers, crew and aircraft. 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"流量控制FLOWCONTROL","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever encountered traffic flow control?","a":"Yes, when I flew from XX to xx I encountered flow control because of military activities over the destination airport, and we joined the holding pattern to hold until we got the clearanceto enter."},{"q":"In which airports are you more likely to encounter flow control?","a":"I think these airports are more likely to encounter flow control: a. large international airports; b. complicated airport with adverse weathers or military activities; C. airport with unexpected situations like delay. What would be the reasons for flow control? There are many reasons for flow control... Aircraft:airspace congestion Airport: weather, military activity Passenger: medical emergency, ViP flight"},{"q":"What can be done to reduce flow control?","a":"Aircraft: apply advanced approach methods and new navigation aids to reduce congestion, improve frequency communication efficiency. improve ATC work efficiency. good communication with ATC; Airport: improve airport management, equipment and SID/STAR procedures."}]},{"title":"失压DEPRESSURIZATION","qa":[{"q":"Can you name some systems on your aircraft?","a":"Sure, flight control systems, hydraulic system, landing gears, automatic flight system pressurization system, power plant, Apu, electrical system, pneumatic system, fuel system, communication andnavigation system."},{"q":"Do you have pressurization system on your aircraft?","a":"No, I fly DA-40/CESSNA172, it is an unpressurized aircraft and it doesn't have pressurization system. But as far as 1 know, big aircraft like B737 and A320 have pressurizationsystems."},{"q":"What are the functions of pressurization system?","a":"Pressurization is used to adjust cabin altitude and make persons on board safe and comfortable during flight over 3000m as the air density is thin and pressure is low at high altitude. Without pressurization above 30oOm, the persons on board will suffer from hypoxia, decompression, incapacitation and so on."},{"q":"What might be the reasons for depressurization?","a":"Slow depressurization: Air-conditioning/pressurization system failure/malfunction; Sealing door/window problem and air leaking. Rapid/explosive depressurization: bomb explosion, windshield/cabin door/window cracking/disintegration. Structural Failure: Failure of a window, door, or pressure bulkhead for example, or in-flight explosion. An in-flight explosion may be due to a system failure, dangerous cargo, or a malicious act consequential on such as an explosive device on board. Pressurization system failure: Malfunction of some part of the pressurization system such as an outflow valve. pressurization control. Deliberate Act: A drastic measure but one which an aircraft captain might consider, for example, as a way of clearing the cabin of smoke."},{"q":"Which one is more dangerous, slow depressurization or rapid depressurization?","a":"Rapid：特性为发生快，氧气、压力流失快，飞行员和乘客来不及反应，短时间失去意识。 尤其在巡航高度。 I think rapiddepressurization ismore dangerous because: It will cause the a/c to lose oxygen and pressure rapidly, crew and pilots will lose consciousness/be incapacitated in a very short time before responding from the situation/wearing oxygen masks. Especially at high level (cruising level). Slow：特性为难以察觉，飞行员和乘客反应过来时已经为时过晚。 Ithink slowdepressurization ismoredangerousbecause: It's hard for pilots and systems to detect and recognize the slow depressurization, and oxygen mask will not jump out during the process of slow depressurization unless the cabin altitude reaches the redline, pilots and POB will gradually lose conscious and fall into a knockout, lose control of aircraft. 太阳神幽灵航班事故 (空调手动模式慢失压) B733, en-route, northwest of Athens Greece, 2005 (On 14 August 2005, a B737-300 aircraft belonging to Helios Airways, crashed near Grammatiko, Greece following the incapacitation of the crew due to Hypoxia)"},{"q":"How do you deal with decompression ? Do you have standard operation","a":"procedures of depressurization? DA-40 during my training we fly below 3,000 (3600m max) meters and we won't encounterdepressurizationproblems. Depressurization procedure in civil jets, pilot needs to put on oxygen mask and emergency descend to MSA which usually is 10,000ft. Report to ATC and request emergency services). 航线飞行员答案 Asfar as I know, the procedures are : a. Put on oxygen masks, establish inner communication, check cabin altitude. b. Emergency descend to below 10,000ft, squawk 7700. C. If situation is worse, make distress call to ATC, carry out checklist. request divert to the nearest suitable airport."},{"q":"When should a pilot make emergency descent?","a":"If a pilot encountered depressurization, pressurization system malfunction. air-conditioning system malfunction, air leaking. onboard fire and smoke, bomb...and so on. weneedto executeemergencydescent."},{"q":"What procedures you should follow when making an emergency descent?","a":"For example, if we countered depressurization and need to make emergency descent, we should ... make decision, report ATC, offset right and make descent, to 3000M MSA. 关于应急下降程序 ICAO Doc 7030 directs the following actions in the event that an aircraft experiences a sudden decompression or a (similar) malfunction requiring an emergency descent: Initiate a turn away from the assigned route or track before initiating the descent (note that in very congested airspace, this may not be advisable and that in some regions, such as the North Atlantic, there are specific contingency procedures to be followed. Certain regions of Europe have, in their AiPs, denoted that an emergency descent should be conducted on their cleared track unless an immediate conflict exists) Advise the appropriate air traffic control unit as soon as possible of the emergency descent Set the transponder code to 7700 and select Emergency Mode on the Automatic Dependent Surveillance (ADS) / Controller Pilot Data Link Communications (CPDLC) equipment as appropriate Turn on all exterior lights Watch for traffic both visually and with reference to ACAS/TCAS (if equipped) Coordinate further intentions with the appropriate ATC unit Ensure that altimeters are set to local QNH or QFE as appropriate. An initial target altitude which is the higher of MORA, MOCA, MEA (as appropriate to routing and aircraft capability), or a specified Company/manufacturer value (often 10,000') will be selected. In mountainous terrain, specific escape routes may be required. Temperature corrections should be applied as appropriate. Unless structural damage is suspected, the aircraft should be descended at or near maximum speed (Vmo) with thrust at idle and flight spoilers or speed brakes extended. If structural damage is suspected, the aircraft should be flown at, or close to, the indicated airspeed (IAs) at which the failure occurred."}]},{"title":"烟雾SMOKE","qa":[{"q":"if you observe smoke in the cockpit during cruise, what will you do?","a":"I fly DA-40, during my training if there is smoke during flight, I will open side window to ventilate smoke, (turn off instrument power, main power and cockpit heating. turn on emergency power) report ATC and declare emergency, request to land at the nearest suitableairport. 航线飞行员答案 a. I will put on oxygen mask and establish inner communication. b. find out where the smell is fromand isolate the source of smell. if I can't find the source, I will descend to MSA and ventilate the cockpit by let in fresh air. d. if the smell is becoming uncontrollable and dense, I will consider diversion and request priority landing. 尽快着随 立即执行以下程序： OFF OFF 恭家信号牌 机妍氧气面草（若高要） ON/100%/EMEF ·如可立即发现明是烟源并可以扑灭： 烟書源 ·如未能立印隔离烟源： 改航 开始 下降列FL100或MEA或最低越障高度开始 ·苦在抗行序的任何时候烟豪汽体点 排烟掌/异味(QRH1.01) 为最大的成协 ·若在抗存值序的任何时候情况变得无法 控制： 立即着陆 2. What are the possible reasons for smoke in the flight deck? a. electrical fire and smoke, overheating, short circuit; b. crews smoking in cockpit; C. lightning strike and electrical failure."}]},{"title":"医疗紧急情况 MEDICALEMERGENCY","qa":[{"q":"What would you do in case of a heart attack passenger on board during flight?","a":"Pilot: I will focus on my control, monitor situation in cabin, keep in touch with the passenger's situation from cabin crew Crew: let the flight attendant to take care of the passenger and give him first aid, CPR, find doctors onboard and heart attack medication, try to oppress the ATC: if it very urgent and serious, I will make a decision to divert and report ATC, requesting diversion to the nearest suitable airport and medical services on arrival. Flight crew response to an on board medical emergency will depend on the nature of the problem, the degree of urgency for medical intervention and the location and phase of fight in which the situation occurs. Unless the situation is considered immediately life threatening. it is normal that no decisions will be taken until more information is available through an first notification and the end of the assessment/diagnosis process to consider their diversion options and the implications of any diversion decision. Consultation with Company operations may be prudent at this stage. Based on the information provided by the flight attendants, an onboard medical professional or a contracted agency, the Captain willmake the decision to either continue the flight to the planned destination or to divert to a closer or otherwise more suitable aerodrome. There are numerous factors that the flight crew should consider when planning and executing a diversion. Given the potential urgency of a medical situation, the crew should start considering their options when first informed that there is a passenger with a medical issue. When choosing a diversion airfield, considerations can be listed under three primary categories; medical, operational and commercial. A partial listing for each of the categories is as follows: Medical How urgent is the situation? Is an immediate landing required or could a more suitable but further diversion be considered. What services will be required? Medical staff at airport, ambulance, hospital., specialist facilities - are they available at or close to the contemplated diversion? Operational Aircraft weight - is the aircraft below maximum landing weight? Will fuel dumping or an overweight landing be required? Aerodrome facilities - is the planned diversion suitable in terms of runway length (for both landing and the subsequent departure), approach capability and parking? Weather - is the weather at the diversion aerodrome suitable? Ground support - does the diversion airfield have the required ground support equipment (GSE) inclusive of steps, baggage handling capability, towing capability etc. for the aircraft type. Customs - if required, are customs services available? Fuel - is fuel available? Crew Duty Day - will the diversion result in an inability for the crew to continue to planned destination?"},{"q":"What would you if a flight crew is unconscious/incapacitated?","a":"Pilot: Take over the control, follow SOP, perform checklist 机组失能检查单 (engage auto-pilot) Crew: let cabin crew take care of him, and find doctors for help ATC: declare emergency report ATC, advise situation, if necessary request vectors and diversion. Airport: medical services upon landing SOP操作程序： 1.如果证实一名飞行员已丧失能力，另一飞行员应接替操纵飞机并检查核实主要的操纵和电门的位置，并："},{"q":"宣布紧急状态，告知ATC\"一名机组失能\"并接通自动驾驶，以减轻工作负荷","a":"3通知ATC，求得ATC协助 4.接通自动驾驶以及飞机处于控制状态后，通知乘务员，尽可能将失能的飞行员移出驾驶舱；如有困难，应将其座椅向后拉到头, 使用肩带将其固定好。 7.通知机场当局或公司签派准备好救护车，落地后尽快抢救丧失能力的飞行员。"},{"q":"When would you declare medical emergency?","a":"In my opinion, when we encountered the following situation, and after a correct assessment and diagnosed by trained medical personnel onboard, we should declare medical emergency: a. crew members become incapacitated/unconscious/severely injured/ill or sick b. Passenger problems such as sick/heart attack/bleeding/unconsciousness C. For any medical related circumstance and poses an urgency of life threatening. I will immediately declare medical emergency. On-Board Medical Equipment An Automated External Defibrillator (AED) is carried on board most commercial aircraft for and are also trained and qualified in CPR (cardiopulmonary resuscitation). A physician's kit or emergency medical kit, containing various drugs and both diagnostic and invasive medical instruments, is carried on board many commercial aircraft. Cabin crew are not trained or qualified to administer drugs and the contents of this kit can only be accessed and administered by a licensed medical practitioner, should one be on board. Typically, a physician's kit will contain some or all of the following (Source: Aerospace Medical Association): Medications Epinephrine 1:1,000 Antihistamine, injectable (inj.) Dextrose 50%, inj. 50 mL (or equivalent) Nitroglycerin tablets or spray Major analgesic, inj. or oral Sedative anticonvulsant, inj. Antiemetic, inj. Bronchial dilator inhaler Atropine, inj. Corticosteroid, inj. Diuretic, inj. Medicationforpostpartumbleeding Normal saline Acetylsalicylic acid for oral use Oral beta-blocker Epinephrine1:10,000 Equipment Stethoscope Sphygmomanometer Airways,oropharyngeal Syringes Needles IV catheters Antiseptic wipes Gloves Sharps disposal box Urinary catheter Intravenous fluid system Venous tourniquet Sponge gauze Tape adhesive Surgical mask Flashlight and batteries Thermometer (nonmercury) Emergency tracheal catheter Umbilical cord clamp Basic life support cards Advanced lifesupportcards"}]},{"title":"旅客问题PASSENGERPROBLEM","qa":[{"q":"What should pilots do if a passenger refuses to turn off the mobile phone/cell","a":"phone? normally it is not my duty, I will let cabin crew and air marshal to handle it, if he causes any trouble, I will get him out and call airport police."}]},{"title":"擦机尾TAIL STRIKE","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever heard about any accidents concerning tail strike?","a":"Yes, last month when we are training the traffic circuit, one of the pilots flared/pitched up too early during landing/touch-and-go, and he caused a tail strike to the a/c, luckily no one was injured. No, but I know tail strike can cause structural damages and injuries"},{"q":"Which phases of flight does a tail strike often happen? why?","a":"Tail strike most likely to happen during takeoff, landing and go around, because the a/c have to rotate to airborne and flare to touchdown 3. What might be the reasons for tail strike? Takeoff: a. Mistrimmed stabilizer.安定面配平错误 b. Rotationat improperspeed.抬前轮速度不正确 C. Excessiverotationrate.抬头率过大 d. Improperuseof the flight director飞行指引使用错误 Landing: a. Unstabilizedapproach，不稳定进近 b. Holding off in theflare.拉平时带杆过长 C. Mishandling of crosswinds.侧风处置不正确 d. Over-rotationduringgo-around复飞时抬头量过大 e, Crosswinds 侧风 Over-rotation during go around, Pre-fight: CG miscalculation and toomuch cargo There are many reasons for tail strike, for example: 外因: strong crosswind, wake turbulence, wind shear, downdraft 人因: pilot improper maneuver/control - unstable approach, not enough rotate speed, rotate too much, flare too early or too much, poor simulator training, lack of skill and awareness 4. What can be done to avoid tail strike? What can a pilot do to avoid tail strike? a. Pilot can do more simulator training and improve flying skills, situation awareness, rotate and flare gently, always have a good control of aircraft, monitor speed, make a good cockpit communication, improve more takeoff and landing experience b. follow SOP (standard takeoff and landing procedures)rotate&flare carefully. C. avoid adverse weathers like turbulence, wind shear etc. d. familiarize a/c system, always have a good control e. good management of CG, calculate and fill correctly in the loading sheet Defenses against the eight principal causal factors listed above include the following: usually knows roughly where the centre of gravity should be and the corresponding trim setting. Test the load sheet numbers against past experience to ensure that the numbers are \"reasonable\" - i.e. do a 'reality' check! Particular care may be required for those conducting Cross Crew Qualification (CCQ) operations; e.g. rotating a heavy A340 Family requires a much firmer back pressure than a light A330 Family. Ensure that the computed speeds are correct for the weight and flap setting. Confirm the accuracy of all FMS entries. Do not initiate rotation before VR. Use the manufacturer's recommended rotation rate. Where available, use a full flight simulator to practice. Do not aggressively rotate the aircraft into the flight director pitch bar. Aim to achieve a speed of V2+10 and the FD commanded pitch at approximately 35' above the runway. The single most important defense is to KNOW the tail strike attitude of your aircraft and NEVER rotate beyond it until you are certain you have lifted off (often the air excessively aggressive rotation rates and gross mass calculation error. This may result in a two stage rotation, but that is better than damaging the aircraft. Do not continue an unstabilised approach. Trim the aircraft in the final approach but not in the flare. Do not excessively hold the aircraft off the runway in an attempt to achieve a smooth touchdown. In gusty crosswind situations, actively control the sink rate. Ensure sufficient thrust is available to compensate for slip induced drag and sink rate. If conditions deteriorate belowanacceptablelevel,GOAROUND! In the event of a late go-around, minimize the pitch change until the engines have spooled and the aircraft is accelerating. Similarly, if permitted by sOPs, do not retract the flaps to the go-around setting until engines have spooled and the aircraft is accelerating. Momentary touchdown during a go-around is not problermatic and the aircraft should not be put at risk by trying to avoid that event. In the event of a seriously bounced landing, as opposed to a little skip, a positive Go Around iaw SOPs (sometimes referred to as a TOGA 10, setting power to TOGA, Pitch attitude to 10 Deg) is important to ensure the ground spoilers do not deploy at the peak of the bounce creating a tail strike and heavy landing."},{"q":"Why is tail strike dangerous?","a":"Any tail strike can cause substantial damage to the aft fuselage of the aircraft which can be time consuming and expensive to repair. Beyond the cost of the repair itself, further expense will be incurred as a result of schedule disruption and the loss of the aircraft for the duration of the repair interval. A tail strike on landing tends to cause more serious damage than the same event during takeoff. In the worst case, the tail can strike the runway before the landing gear touches down, thus absorbing large amounts of energy for which it is not designed. The aft pressure bulkhead (a dome-shaped structural wall at the rear of the fuselage responsible for maintaining passenger cabin pressure) is often damaged as a result. There are several catastrophic failure at a later point in time. In the case of Boeing 747 accident, an improperly repaired pressure bulkhead, that had been damaged by a tail strike, lead to the in-flight loss of the vertical stabilizer and subsequent crash of the aircraft seven years later. 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"发动机特情ENGPROBLEMS","qa":[{"q":"If you encounter engine problems in flight, what will you do?","a":"If Iencountered engine problem like engine ice ingestion,I will immediately change my level to avoid icing and turn on anti-ice function, check my engine conditions, if everything goes well,I will continueflight."},{"q":"If engine fire occurs in flight, what may be expected in the following flight?","a":"it may cause engine failure, reduced thrust, power and performance, and cause air conditioning system malfunction due to insufficient air source and power source, pressurization and electrical system malfunction Rejected Take Off - if the fire is identified prior to V1, the crew might abandon the take-off during the take-off roll this will normally be communicated to ATC at the sametime. Emergency landing - if the fire occurs after V1 or during any other airborne phase of the flight, the crew will normally complete the take-off and carry out an emergency landing at the nearest suitable airfield. Engine failure - a malfunction, or Uncontained Engine Failure, associated with fire could render the engine inoperative. The emergency procedures followed will depend whether the aircraft is single or multi engined. For a single-engined aircraft, an immediate landing will be unavoidable whether or not a suitable airfield is available. Rate of descent - in the event of an enroute engine fire three descent scenarios are possible. If the fire drill is successful and the fire is out, assuming that there is some distance to the diversion airfield, the crew are most likely to initiate a \"drift down\" profile resulting in a low rate of descent. If the fire is out and the decision has been made to divert to an enroute airfield or continue to planned destination, the descent rate will be more or less normal for the aircraft type. If the fire is uncontrollable, the flight crew are likely to initiate a high speed/maximum rate descent and divert to nearest airfield. Smoke in the Cockpit - possible intrusion of smoke into the cockpit or the cabin, due to bleed air system contamination, with the associated communication problems due to sound distortion caused by donning of oxygen masks. Pressurisation problems - due to the engine fire/engine shutdown, the aircraft might not be able to stay pressurized. In this scenario, depressurization is likely to be gradual but depending upon the aircraft type and any collateral damage caused by the fire or uncontained engine failure, the depressurization could be rapid."},{"q":"What are the procedures for engine fire during flight?","a":"there is only one engine in DA-40, if it is on fire, I will (turn off cockpit heating and emergency fuel valve) use fire extinguisher b. open side window find alternate airport as soon as possible and emergency landing (with engine shutting down) d. informATCandrequestairportassistance High workload - Such scenarios are associated with intense workload; the crew will carry out the appropriate engine on fire drills. Engine shutdown - Normally the fire drills require shutting down the engine and cutting off fuel and electrical supply to the engine. Following this, extinguishant is fired into the engine and a visual inspection of the affected engine is carried out by a member of the cabin or flight crew (if possible) It should be noted that an engine on fire could sill produce thrust; it is a critical element to consider when dealing with engine fire emergencies on single engine aircraft. In addition, it should be noted also that historically there have been cases of improper identification of the problematic engine followed by wrong engine shutdown. Announcing the problem - the crew will communicate the problem to ATC. Non-standard phraseology should be avoided; an emergency (MAYDAY) or urgency(PANPAN)callshouldbemade. Seeking information and deciding on course of action - the crew will need any information available regarding adjacent aerodromes and weather conditions if they decide to proceed to and land at the nearest suitable aerodrome. Engine Fire Extinguishing two zones '1' and '2' for the purposes of fire protection. Two electrically operated extinguishers containing Halon 1301 or, in newer aircraft, HFCs (Hydrofluorocompounds), are available to each engine. These are sometimes installed in the nose cowling of each nacelle but may also be located within the fuselage and \"shared\" by the engines of a twin engine aircraft or be located in a dry bay in the wing and be \"shared\" by the engines on that wing of a multiengine aircraft. When activated, the contents of the extinguisher bottle are discharged into engine Zone 1, the engine fan assembly. Fires in Zone 2, the engine core, are extinguished by shutting down the engine. The flight crew engine fire drill includes shutting off the fuel and hydraulic fluid supplies to the engine concerned and the supply of oil, the other potentially readily-inflammable substance, is rapidly diminished since it is an isolated supply in each engine/APU. Activation of engine fire bottles is normally also advised as a precautionary measure whenever an engine shutdown dill is carried out because of severe damage. APu Fire extinguishers are activated by the flight crew in the same way as engine extinguishers - by manual selection upon receipt of a fire warning - when airborne, but automatically, and with accompanying automatic APU shutdown, in the case of fire detection duringground running. 5. If pilots make a decision to reject takeoff due to engine fire, what should they be careful about? a. remaining runway length and current rolling speed, and apply a good takeoff abortion procedure b. try to keep/align the a/c nose along the runway centerline C. follow SOP to carry out engine fire procedure, extinguish the fire and report ATC request airport fire service,when a/c stops evacuate all passengers d. share workloads in takeoff abortion procedure and cooperate well with copilot e. askforappropriateassistance fromATC andairport shutdown all engines and electrical power and evacuation if needed 6. What are the consequences if the flight crew discharges the fire extinguisher by mistake? Normally this won't happen, because the fire extinguisher button is located at designated position and protected by a button cover, pilot also needs to do cross check to discharge the extinguisher. If ENG fire extinguisher is mistakenly discharged, the affected ENG will lose power or evenfail and sufferdamages"},{"q":"What is the procedure to discharge the fire extinguisher for the engine?","a":"First, crew need to find out which engine is on fire by monitoring instrument or visual check, then put throttle to IDLE, shut down the affected engine, cut off the fuel supply and discharge the engine fire extinguisher 8. [ Do you know any engine parameters? Can you list some examples? Oil temperature, EGT, engine rotation speed N1, N2, FOB and so on...RPM= rotation per minute,oil pressure, oil temperature 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"导航与失效NAVIGATIONANDFAILURE","qa":[{"q":"What navigational aids do you use? What kinds of approach can you make?","a":"during training we use VOR, NDB, DME, ILS, GPS precision approach: ILS approach, precision radar approach, microwave landing system approach non-precision approach: NDB approach, VOR approach, NDB/VOR and DME approach Are the ILS approach and vOR approach the same? What are the difference? a. No. ILS approach is precision approach while VOR approach is non-precision approach. b. We have DA(Decision Altitude)in ILS approach and MDA(Minimum Descent Altitude)in VOR approach. C. ILS has glide slope, localizer and marker beacon but VOR only has VOR course."},{"q":"If you encounter navaids display failure because of electrical power, what are you","a":"If there is navigation display malfunction because of electrical failure, we will use standby instrument and request radar vectors."},{"q":"If you encounter navigation failure, how can ATC help you?","a":"安全间隔调配separationallocation 空域引导服务guidance/vector 情报服务informationservice 协调应急服务airportemergencyassistance a. ATC can make enough separation for our aircraft to avoid conflicting traffic, and give us clearance to divert b. ATC can vector/guide us to the alternate airport C. ATC can provide necessary information of weather and alternate airport d. ATC can contact the alternateairport emergency services for us Job of ATC separation of the aircraft from other traffic providing diversion clearance or facilitating delaying tactics by providing vectors or a holdingclearance prioritization of the emergency aircraft for landing allowing for a long final if requested assigning a discreteradiofrequency when possible advising the airport emergency services and all concerned parties in accordance with localprocedures/protocols providing any information that might be requested by the crew such as weather, type of approaches available,runway length and other pertinent aerodrome details"},{"q":"If you get lost in the air, what will you do?","a":"Itrarelyhappens, If it happens in IFR flights, I would maintain present heading, report to ATC and request radarvectors,andinDA4oNGwewilluseGPSduringflight. If it happens in VFR flight, I will climb to obtain a better visual outside, try to reduce engine power to save enough fuel, observe landmarks and compare with my chart to identify positions,reportto ATC ofmy situationand followguidance."}]},{"title":"跑道入侵RUNWAY INCURSION","qa":[{"q":"What is (the definition of) runway incursion?","a":"Generally speaking, during takeoff rolling, if there is any aircraft, vehicle, personnel, animal present/enter into the runway without permission/clearance will be considered as runwayincursion Any occurrence at an aerodrome involving the incorrect presence of an aircraft, vehicle or person on the protected area of a surface designated for the landing and takeoff of aircraft. 2. Have you heard about any accidents of runway incursion? Yes, I know a disaster of aviation happened in Tenerife in 1977. Two B747 collided with each other, during the KLM takeoff rolling, the PanAm hasn't vacated runway, this accident caused 583fatalities/deaths, only 61 survivals."},{"q":"What might be the reasons for runway incursion?","a":"(PILOT: poor communication with ATC, not following instructions, not reading back carefully, non-standard RT, poor preflight preparation, not familiar with airport, not enough simulator training, lack of situation awareness and skill (ATC) wrong clearance to pilot, wrong clearance to the vehicle/personnel to the occupied runway.non-standard RT, heavy workload. AIRPORT. low visibility and heavy fog. and these will cause potential runway incursion and threaten/endanger flight safety. WeathePLowvisibilitymay increase the chanceof flight crewbecoming disorientated and unsure of their position whilst taxying. Low visibility is also likely to restrict a controller's ability to identify and follow aircraft visually so that cross-checking a reported aircraft position with its actual location may become impossible unless Surface Movement Radar is available. Aerodrome design If, as a consequence of aerodrome design, aircraft have to cross active runways to move between their take off or landing runway and their parking position, the likelihood of runway incursions is increased. This risk may be reduced if the LRST identifies the Runway Hotspots thereby created and effective risk mitigation is developed and applied. Operation with intersecting active runways is also likely to require careful consideration to ensure that the inherently increased risk of conflict is adequately managed. Multiple Simultaneous Line-ups Use of Multiple Line-ups for a series of aircraft departures from the same runway from different entry positions may increase the potential for runway collision. Conditional Clearances If conditional clearances are used, the risk consequent upon any error in their issue or actioning may be increased because of errors in aircraft identification by adjacent aircraft. The chances of such errors are increased if aircraft livery does not readily correspond to the RTF callsign being used; this is sometimes the result of airline alliance livery policies or the ad hoc operational substitution of leased-inaircraft. Simultaneous Use of Intersecting Runways Unless ATM SOPs are carefully formulated and rigorously applied, use of intersecting runways can significantly raise the risk of both runway incursions and Loss of Separation between aircraft near the ground and aircraft on the ground. At some airports where intersecting runways are used, especially in the USA, Land and Hold Short Operations are part of normal procedures. These are considered by some non-US aircraft operators to introduce an unacceptable level of additional risk; consequently, their flight crews are instructed to decline offers of such clearances. Late Issue of or late changes to Departure Clearances This may lead to a temporary lapse in flight crew situational awareness if an attempt is then be made to set up or modify the FMS for departure whilst it is taxing. Phraseology Use of Non-Standard Phraseology or non-adherence to Standard Phraseology can lead to clearance confusion and misunderstanding between flight crew and controllers. In particular the standard expression \"Taxi into position and hold\" used in the USA until 2010 in place of the ICAO Phrase \"Line up and wait\" is worthy of note. Concurrent Use of More than One Language for ATC communications. At some international airports, locally-based users are permitted to communicate in the local language whereas foreign aircraft do so in English. Depending on the nature of the local language and the language sills of the visiting flight crew, this may have the effect of significantly reducing their awareness of the relative position of other traffic. English Language Competence Despite the introduction by ICAO of a system of validating competence in Aviation English, instances of pilots whose native language is not Englishmisunderstanding taxi clearances still occur, (Workload Pilot Workload. Shortly after landing, flight crew have to orientate themselves quickly in respect of their actual position in relation to taxiways and the airport layout.After clearing the landing runway,they also have to reconfigureaircraft systems inaccordancewiththeAfter Landing Checks and may receive detailed taxi instructions from ATC, Similar levels of workload may occur prior to departure while the flight crew are concurrently carrying out tasks including configuring the aircraft systems ready for clearance instructions from ATC, checking unfamiliar departure procedures, etc. Under these circumstances of high workload, a temporary loss of situational awareness or communications confusion are more likely to occur. Controller Workload Controllers handling multiple aircraft movements and handovers have relatively little time available for monitoring individual aircraft to confirm that they are taxiing in accordance with their clearances. Distraction. This is the immediate cause of many incursions, although the context in which it occurs is often of more direct relevance to effective risk mitigation 4. What can a pilot do to avoid runway incursion a. good communication with ATC - follow ATC instructions strictly and readback correctly. use standard radio communication, confirm with ATC if not sure b. make good preflight preparation, familiarize with airport chart and procedures C. have a good visual check during taxi, if taxi in fog, taxi with low speed and turn on all lights. d. monitor frequency carefully and listen to PIREP e. simulator training, improve situation awareness and skill Maintenance of situation awareness by flight crew and others using the maneuvering area, specifically in respect of their own location in relation to active runways, and that of other aircraft and vehicles relative to active runways. Maintenance of situation awareness by TWR and GND ATCOs in respect of aircraft andvehicledisposition andmovementsneartoactiverunways Flight Crew use of the TCAS display to provide situational awareness of other aircraft both in the airand on theground. EffectiveflightcrewuseofappropriatefeaturesofRAAS if installed Where instalied, effective procedures for the use of Enhanced Vision Systems (EvS) for improved awareness of runwayoccupancy Use of ICAO Standard Phraseology at alltimes to minimize any risk of clearance confusion. Presence of ICAO standard Runway Markings and Taxiway Surface Markings and Signs Presence of ICAO standard Runway Lighting and Taxiway Lighting including the installation of Runway Status Lights (RWSL) and Runway Holding Point Lighting. Controllers working traffic where theflight crew are not native speakers of English should pay particular attention to their speech clarity, use only standard phraseology and make a particular effort to closely monitor readbacks of taxi clearances."},{"q":"What consequences does runway incursion have?","a":"there will be deaths and injuries if incursion develops to a collision, the consequence of runway incursion is usually severe, aircraft collision or even explosion,"}]},{"title":"火情 IN-FLIGHT FIRE","qa":[{"q":"If you encounter cabin fire, how can you handle it?","a":"Pilot: we should put on oxygen mask, execute emergency descent Passenger&crew: let cabin crew and passengers use fire extinguisher cylinder/bottle to put out the fire ATC: declare distress to ATC and request priority landing Airport: request airport fire and medical assistance on arrival if necessary, execute emergency evacuation after landing"}]},{"title":"操 纵 面 系 统 和 失 控 FLIGHT CONTROL","qa":[{"q":"Please describe the positions/locations of primary flight control surfaces","a":"Rudder is (hinged to/located at) at the trailing edge of vertical stabilizer Elevator is (hinged to/located at) at the trailing edge of horizontal stabilizer Aileron is (hinged to/located at) at the trailing edge of outboard (outer board) wings 3. Can you explain the functions of flight control surfaces? a. Elevatorcontrolspitch b. Ruddercontrolsyaw C. Aileron controls roll d. Flaps increase lift and drag"},{"q":"What are the possible reasons for the aircraft to be out of control?","a":"A/c: system failure such as control system failure, hydraulic system failure and electrical systemfailure PILOT: poor simulator training, poor flying skill, poor situation awareness WEATHER: adverse weathers such as severe icing, turbulence, wind shear, bird strike."},{"q":"What are the possible consequences of no flaps/flapless landing?","a":"aircraft will encounter high speed landing and heavy landing, cause landing bounce and tire burst or even overrun the runway and veer off the runway Also cause injuries to crew and passengers due to landing impact"},{"q":"Can you describe the flaps?","a":"Yes, it's part of secondary flight control surfaces, located at the inboard trailing edge of each wing, it's a high lift device and it can increase drag and lift during takeoff and landing"},{"q":"What are the flaps setting for takeoff according to your company sOP?","a":"What are the flaps setting for takeoff and landing? B737 when we takeoff the flap setting is 10 degrees, when we land and gear down we set FLAPS15 A320 when we takeoff we set FLAPS 1 after airborne, and set FLAPS 0 for acceleration to higher altitude climbing. When we land, we set FLAPS 3 and FLAPS FULL for a need of lift and speeddecreasing. DA40 has two FLAPS settings, during takeoff we will set FLAPS 1, during landing we set FLAPS 2,"},{"q":"What should you pay attention to when extending or retracting the flaps?","a":"before: we should pay attention to the airspeed and make extension and retraction accordingtochecklist. after: we should confirm the flaps are in designated position 9. How to make a success landing without flaps or partial flaps? a. pilot should monitor speed and control the aircraft precisely. b. properly reduce thrust to reduce landing high speed C. executeflapless landingchecklist andmaneuver d. request the longest runway and inform crew and passengers for impact"},{"q":"If you lose flight control, what would you do?","a":"PILOT: maintain my a/c(have a good maneuver, maintain heading) and try to have good control of my a/c, try to glide my aircraft to destination, follow sOP and perform checklist (for example, if flaps are failed I will do according to the checklist) ATC/AIRPORT: if I cannot fix the problem (if the situation is worse/deteriorating), I will inform ATc and request airport assistance. declare distress, prepare for possible forced landing or ditching. after landing, execute evacuation 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"中断起飞TAKEOFF REJECTION","qa":[{"q":"What arethepossible reasonsfora pilottoaborttakeoff?","a":"what may cause a pilot to reject takeoff? Before V1 if the following abnormal situation happens we need to abort takeoff: Master warning light lluminate,system failure, abnormal noise or vibration,tire problems,acceleration is too slow,takeoff configuration problem,fire warning, engine failure, engine exceeds its limitations, wind shear warning, and any adverse condition significantly affecting the safety of flight. What will you do when the warning alerts are triggered during takeoff rolling? it depends. a. if thespeed isblowV1,we should aborttakeoffimmediately+(中断程序) b. if the speed is over V1, we should continue takeoff + (后续 SOP) 3. What are the procedures for takeoff rejection/abortion? I am a student pilot, I fly DA-40 / DA-42, if we want to abort takeoff, we will pull/reduce throttle (IDLE), when speed goes down, we press brakes pedal to stop 航线飞行员答案 For big aircraft, if the speed is below V1, the captain makes the decision to reject takeoff, he will call\"' stop\", reduce throttle/pull throttle, apply maximum brake, deploy speed brakes andthrustreverse. (keep/align the nose along the centerline, when it stops, report to ATC and make PA to passengers)"},{"q":"What may happen if the pilot rejects takeoff after V1?","a":"If the pilot rejects takeoff after V1, PlLoT: the pilot won't have enough time to execute the takeoff rejection procedure, hard to stop the a/c due to high speed A/C: there will be insufficient/not enough RwY to fully stop the a/c, and the a/c will overrun/veer off the RwY and suffer structural damages to gears... PASSENGERS&CREWS:injuries"}]},{"title":"紧急与遇险URGENCYAND DISTRESS","qa":[{"q":"When is it necessary for you to make an in-flight emergency descent?","a":"In myopinion/As farasIknow/According tomyknowledge... When we encounter depressurization, air-conditioning system failure, fire and smoke.. we need to execute emergency descent for flight safety concerns"},{"q":"Have you encountered any urgency or distress situation? If yes, can you say","a":"something in detail? if no, can you give me an example? No, I haven't encountered any urgency and distress situation, but I know there was a disaster in Tenerife that happened in 1977, two B737 collided each other and caused almost 600 fatalities and only 61 survivals. That was the worst distress situation I ever heard of."},{"q":"What could you do in case of an emergency? Give me an example","a":"In case of an emergency, a pilot has a lot procedures to execute, For example, if we encountered depressurization, we need to put on oxygen masks execute emergency descent, follow SOP,perform checklist, contact ATC and request landing as soon as possible, execute emergency evacuation upon landing. What kind of supports do you need from ATc in case of emergency? a. ATC can make enough separation for our aircraft to avoid conflicting traffic, and give us clearance in emergency b. ATC can vector us to the alternate airport c. ATC can provide necessary information of weather and alternate airport d. ATC can contact the ground personnel for emergency services Job of ATC separation of the aircraft from other traffic providing diversion clearance or facilitating delaying tactics by providing vectors or a holdingclearance requested assigning a discrete radio frequency when possible advising theairport emergency services and all concernedparties in accordance with localprocedures/protocols providing any information that might be requested by the crew such as weather. type of approaches available, runway length and other pertinent aerodrome details"},{"q":"What should you do when you hear one aircraft making a distress transmission","a":"duringflight? Stop transmitting if the distress aircraft is communicating with ATC Relay the message if the distress aircraft is making a broadcast and ask allstations to pass themessage for him."}]},{"title":"地面相撞GROUNDCOLLISION","qa":[{"q":"What may be the possible reasons for ground collisions?","a":"Pilot: poor simulator training. poor preflight preparation, unfamiliar with airport, non-standard phraseology ATC: issue wrong instruction, heavy workload AIPORT:lowvisibility,heavyfog Aircraft:mechanical malfunction,steering Failure of stakeholders to proactively ensure that aircraft are not involved in collisions with other aircraft when moving on the maneuvering area, and that the jet engine exhaust gases from large aircraft do not create a threat for small aircraft; Failure to ensure safe parking and docking of aircraft; Failure to proactively mitigate the risk of impact damage to parked aircraft or to conducted prior to any further flight operations; Failure to provide adequate signage, markings and lighting that enable aircraft flight crews to comply with taxy clearances; and, Failure to train - at a level of quality consistent with aviation professionals - the various types of unlicensed contractors and subcontractors who conduct and supervise aircraft ground-handling tasks on the maneuvering area and/or in the vicinity of an aircraft parking stand or gate. 2. What damages might ground collision bring to the a/c? a. Ground collision is very dangerous, it will cause structural damages to a/c such as wings, engine, landing gear damages and even fire and explosion b. It will also cause crew and passenger injuries and endanger their flight safety. C. It may cause long ground delays or holding in the air Scenarios Aircraft takes taxi route with potential conflict after an incorrect ATC clearance: clearance; Aircraft takes incorrect taxi route after a non-conformance with ATC clearance due to spatial/positional confusion; Aircraft incorrectly enters onto maneuvering area after a non-conformance with ATC clearance due to spatial/positional confusion; Aircraft takes incorrect taxi route after non-conformance with ATC clearancedue to a misinterpretation [of clearance] or [misheard] clearance Aircraftincorrectly enters onto maneuvering area after non-conformance with ATC clearance due to a misinterpretation [of clearance] or [misheard] clearance; Aircraft takes incorrect taxi route due to poor [ crew resource management (CRM) ] or [forgotten] planned action; and, Aircraft incorrectly enters onto maneuvering area runways without ATC clearance due to poor CRM or [forgotten] planned action."},{"q":"If you collide with a ground truck while taxi, what would you do?","a":"PILOT&A/C: I will stop the a/c immediately, shutdown all engines ATC: Report to ATC and request maintenance If serious, PASSENGER&CREW: execute emergency evacuation AIRPORT: Request airport assistance such as fire and medical services In your opinion, how can we reduce ground collision incidents? a. make a good preflight preparation-be familiar with airport, taxi routes b. good communication with ATC, monitor frequency carefully-Use standard RT, follow instruction and read back correctly C. Taxi slowly and turn all lights on in fog d. Make good visual scan and keep distance with traffic e. If unsure the instruction, confirm with ATC Simulator training, keep situation awareness, improve skill Direct visual detection of conflict on the maneuvering area, Indirect detection using remote camera displays, Detection following a pilot/vehicle driver report, Detection using basic surface-movement radar, Detection using an advanced surface movement guidance and control system Detection after an alert from an Integrated Tower Working Position (ITWP) or from aerodrome infrastructure that detects aircraft entry onto the runway (e.g., magnetic loops or lasers); Assuming that ATC maintains situational awareness and issues a correct taxi clearance - and the aircraft flight crew complies with clearances or standard routings - the highest risk of wing tip collision occurs when multiple aircraft are holding or taxing in the maneuvering area (e.g. near a runway entry point, changing the queuing order (especially at night) or moving without benefit of visible taxiway centerlines; Flight crews of swept-wing aircraft must stay alert to the physical clearance during a turn in which the wing tip describes an arc greater than the normal wingspan due to the geometry of the aircraft and the arranigernent of the landing gear. Air traffic controllers should be attentive and proactive in providing progressive taxi instructions if flight crews seem unfamiliar with the aerodrome maneuvering area or have difficulty because of a particularly unintuitive procedure or a temporarily complexlayout 5. Who do you think should be responsible for runway incursion? I think both pilots and controllers should be responsible for runway incursion. If the pilots misunderstood the ATC instruction, the pilot should be responsible for runway incursion. If the controller issues wrong instruction to the pilots, the controller should be responsible for runway incursion. And both of them should be aware of the wingspan clearance, also watch out for any other movement on the ground when operating. 相关图片描述"}]}]},{"title":"四、程序、手册、飞行 PROCEDURE &","subs":[{"title":"飞行前准备PREFLIGHTPREPARATION","qa":[{"q":"What is the primary purpose of flight planning","a":"The primary purpose of preflight planning is to make good preflight preparation and ensure the flight safety. As we know, many accidents were caused by bad preflight preparation."},{"q":"What would you do to prepare a flight?","a":"What should a pilot do in preflight preparation? 预先准备：查航班，查天气，查NOTAM，查起降、备降机场航图，查飞机信息 直接准备：开小会，查证件，查航行资料，核对放行计划，讨论沿途天气 飞行实施：绕机检查，驾驶舱准备 a. Days before flight, we will check online information of the assigned route, weather, aircraft and airport. b. Before enter airport, we will have a briefing in preparation(briefing) room, and check licenses, documents, charts, and other material, also have discussion about weather en-route. C. Before start up, we will make walk around inspection (check fuel, wings, engines, leakages.) and cockpit inspection(check cockpit settings, documents...) 航线飞行员答案preflight There are many things we often do during preflight preparation. First, we will get the weather information and airport information such as departure airport, destination airport and alternate airport. Second, we will calculate fuel consumption, weight and balance. Third, we will make and file the flight plan. Fourth, we will make preflight briefing. Fifth, we will make preflight inspection to ensure the aircraft meets MEL requirement. We will walk around the aircraft to check the engines, the landing gear, the flight control surfaces, the fuel and so on. Then will have the cockpit check."},{"q":"What do you do in the briefing room before flying?","a":"I am a student pilot, before my flight training. we will discuss about the weather and training subject with our flight instructor, and conditions of the aircraft we are flying that day. 航线飞行员答案Briefing before departure, every crew gather/enter in the briefing room, and get documents (NOTAM, flight plan, weather) from dispatcher. The captain will give a briefing to crews, and discuss weather, alternate airport, task sharing,procedures and etc. (forexample,signsofhijack and evacprocedures)"},{"q":"What would you do in the walk around inspection before flight?","a":"航线飞行员答案groundinspection Ground check, we mainly check the external parts of a/c (fuselage, wings, control surfaces, pitot tube, gears etc.) in clockwise direction, start from left forward cabin door Cockpit Check, we confirm normal&emergency equipment are available, check flight documents are updated, check the refueling, enter flight route data into FMC, and do some tests: instrument, signal, warning system, weather radar.(Airbus - dark cockpit philosophy),after this we enter data into FMS"},{"q":"How long does it take to do a preflight preparation online?","a":"it depends, usually it takes about 30 mins to go through all the information online, but if I am not familiarizing with this flight, it will take longer correspondingly."},{"q":"Do you make briefing with cabin crew? what you will do?","a":"We will have safety briefing with cabin crew in the briefing room before flying. First, we will discuss the weather information. Second, we will keep in mind our duties and responsibilities during flight. Third, we will discuss the possible abnormal or emergency situations and procedures. it will improve cooperation of crew members, increase communication and ensure flight safety."},{"q":"What information or data do you need to obtain before departure?","a":"Thereare lots of information we should obtain: a. weatherinformation:alternate/destination/takeoffairport b. route information: flight plan, NOTAM, C. aircraft information: crew, a/c condition, company limitation, loading sheet, FOB,weight andbalance d. airport information: airport chart, aeronautical chart e. training program"},{"q":"Do you think walk around check is necessary/important?","a":"Yes it is very important and it is part of our SOP列举反例 we must check aircraft condition before flight and we can find out many abnormal situations such as fuel contamination, ice ingestion, fluid leakage, pitot tube blockage and so on, it's critical to our flight safety 9. Whoseresponsibility is it to perform the walk-around inspection for every flight? According to flight manual Captain /the pilot-in-command has the duty to do the walk-around inspection I am a student pilot, for each flight, we will inspect the condition of a/c and our flight instructor will confirm our check."},{"q":"Which part do you think that needs to be checked carefully during walk around","a":"check? and why? pitot tube: foreign object(ice) will block it and cause instrument inaccuracy. control surfaces: they could be jammed and cause control problems. landing gear: there might be fluid leakage, flat tire fuel tank: fuel contamination"},{"q":"Is there a specific route for a walk-around inspection? What is it? And what's the","a":"purpose? yes we start from left wing to check aircraft inclockwisedirection It is a procedure in SOP and the purpose is to avoid human errors, increase work efficiency and ensure flight safety 航线飞行员答案 Yes, walk-around inspection starts from left forward cabin door and goes around the a/c in clockwise direction. It is a procedure in sOP and the purpose is to avoid human errors, increase work efficiency and ensure flight safety"},{"q":"If you find something abnormal/wrong during your preflight check, what action","a":"would you take? If I find anything abnormal/wrong during my pre-flight preparation, I wil... ask the maintenance staff/engineer/mechanics/ground staff to take a look (and check MEL and decide whether I should continue my flight or change another a/c)"},{"q":"When you are performing the walk-around inspection in an extremely cold","a":"weather, is there anything that you need to be careful about? In cold weather, I will be careful about ... Ice accumulation/buildup on wings, it might damage the a/c and endanger flight safety. pitot tube, it might be jammed and cause instrument inaccuracy. 相关图片描述 InspaxtionRoute (二） 载重平衡单LOADINGSHEET .8-19692SAM CXA8587 U-PR208137Z 编9，/01136 PRINTER MSG 载平衡表格式与说明 版次，第5版 XMNMFMF670137 湖门M空XIAMENAIR iTH期,2030 01-09 AGH 4NB-1969/MA093A ACARS载量平表格式与说明 QUXMNMFMF-1RA1202793? XIAMEN AIRLINES LOADSHEET EDNO81 ALL UEIGHTS IN KG DA1E 11081.9) 11182 DATE:07APR20.TIHE:B935AGENTA05507 EIR + A )) .95423 HIGD (R)) FL1GHT:HE850Z/07APR28 DGION F123192 (XI(L/RAR) 2 ≤-9 XMNNKGBL9E9 UERSI0N:J8Y162 CRE:3/S/0 CAB:8 (Ga) WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION BiSDiTiun r 4) HAX TRAFFIC PAYLOAD 18982 Dou DOI: 042724 42.9F 11. 16 PAYLOAO BLKDa/17 7885 () ZF HACZFUE18.11 83160 2x.7 MZFH 61688 S.26 TOF H890 7491 81:29 59820 MACTOUI. 2148 34t HTOU 77546 TRIP FUEL 3808 HACLAUI LDU 54220 18.55 6(611 HLDU 65317 FLAPS1.AND SIAB TO FLAPS 10.15 AND25 STAB TO PASSENGER 5048 8418/0 SEATING w3/81 08/5160138 1757 1702/600 LOAD IN CO 008/E UNDERLOAD BEFORE LHE 14097 LKCTOTAL 51113 (116D NKG FREN 48POS 88RG3579 BAGP3OLE SENDNUH SENDER FANGXIAPING MFPZ 遥行平品"},{"q":"What's the use of loading sheet? What kinds of information are included in the","a":"loading sheet? It is used for weight and balance control, with the loading sheet, pilots can know the weight and CG of the aircraft to calculate takeoff data It includes: flight number, POB, weight of cargo, FOB, baggage, CG, takeoff weight and so on."},{"q":"How do you get the loading sheet? Which department is responsible for the","a":"provision of theloading sheet? Thedispatching office is responsible forproviding the loading sheet loading sheet can be sentto us by ACARS,or the ground staff pass it to us."},{"q":"Which department is responsible for providing the loading sheet to flight crew?","a":"Operational center; weight and balance office"}]},{"title":"手册MANUAL","qa":[{"q":"What kinds of manuals or handbooks are there in the cockpit of the aircraft you","a":"fly? 《飞机飞行手册》AFM(Aircraft/AirplaneFlightManual) 《机组操作手册》FCOM(Flight CrewOperations Manual) 《快速检查单》QRH(QuickReferenceHandbook) 《飞机最低设备放行清单》MEL(MinimumEquipmentList) 航图AeronauticalChart 进近图ApproachChartetc."},{"q":"Do you often consult these manuals in flight? Are they convenient to use?","a":"Yes, for example, before flight, we consult the Aircraft Flight Manual to know the performance of the aircraft. In normal situations, we will use normal checklists and in emergency situations we will use QRH and MEL to ensure flight safety."},{"q":"Is it possible that these paper manuals be replaced by the electronic files?","a":"it is possible, nowadays airlines prefer paperless working (EFB), all the documents and materials are saved in the electronic devices, it is more portable and convenient to read and to be updated, it reduces cost and saves time. It is not possible, because paper manuals can be used anytime even in case of electrical failures."},{"q":"Which one do you prefer to read, paper manuals or electronic files?","a":"I prefer electronic files because all documents can be found in one device, and it's portable/convenient, this saves me plenty of time. I prefer paper manuals because electronic devices may be malfunctioned sometimes."}]},{"title":"危险品DANGEROUSGOODS","qa":[{"q":"Have you ever received any training concerning dangerous goods transportation?","a":"What was involved in training? I am a student pilot and I haven't back to my company yet, but I know the dangerous goodstraining: According to CAAC's regulation, civil pilots should receive such training every two years, cargo pilots will receive training every year. b. In the training, we will learn regulation, transportation and classification/category of dangerous goods"},{"q":"What topics do you discuss during dangerous goods training? Give me an example","a":"We learn regulations and emergency procedures For example, we will learn the regulations for carrying lithium batteries and we learn how to deal with the situation in case of emergency"},{"q":"Can you tell me some names of dangerous goods?","a":"for example: alcohol, battery, fireworks, oxygen, dry ice, paint, lighters, weapons 按类别分类 In Category: Substances that may cause fire, damage and injury are defined as dangerous goods such as radioactive, flammable, explosive, toxic or corrosive/erosive objects"},{"q":"Why is liquid not allowed to carry into the cabin?","a":"Because some liquid is easy to leak and hard to be detected by security scanner and visual inspection, some terrorist will make liquid bomb for hijacking for example, fuel, oil, liquor/wine, gasoline are flammable, if leakage happens it might cause fire and smoke, pose threats to/endanger/threaten flight safety"},{"q":"Is lithium battery a dangerous good? What threats are they likely to cause to flight","a":"safety? Yes, lithiumbattery isdangerous good It may cause fire, smoke and explosion, and the fire is hard to be put out by dry powder injuries"},{"q":"If there's a leakage of a dangerous liquid substance in flight, what would you do?","a":"Leakage of dangerous fluid substance is very dangerous, it can cause fire and smoke. Pilot: We need to follow SOP and execute checklist to handle it ATC/airport: report to ATC, request landing and fire services on arrival. Passenger/crew: stay away from the liquid, first aid to injuries, consider emergency evacuationafterlanding"},{"q":"Dose your company provide guidelines for identifying or handling of dangerous","a":"goods? Do you think it is necessary? Yes, we have Dangerous Goods Transportation Manual and Dangerous Goods Training Program. They provide us guidance to deal with dangerous goods transportation"},{"q":"Do you think pilot should have some knowledge of dangerous goods","a":"transportation? yes, pilots need to know how to identify and how to deal with the dangerous goods to ensuresafety."}]},{"title":"机场保全AIRPORTSECURITY","qa":[{"q":"What is the purpose of airport security check?","a":"the main purpose of security check is to find out dangerous goods and make sure no dangerous goods into cabin, reduce cabin crew's workload and ensure the flight safety. smuggling, bio-hazard"},{"q":"What agencies and personnel are involved in ensuring the safety of airport?","a":"airport security department, airport police, airport fire department, emergency department and all security staff are responsible for the safety of airport"},{"q":"Is it important for airports to strengthen the security check?","a":"Yes, we should strengthen security check to prevent terrorist attack, bomb, guns and explosiveitemstoensuresafety. What can airport and airlines do to improve/strengthen the airport security? a. airport and airlines should apply more advanced security equipment and devices. b. executemoretrainingstoground staff C. have a better cooperation with police andfiredepartments d. Management improvement increase public security awareness of passengers and staff"},{"q":"Do you think the ground security has improved in the last decade?","a":"Yes, Ground security has improved a lot on their equipment and security equipment is more developed nowadays. besides security staffis well trained and the number of security staffis increased 6. With the development of civil aviation, do you have any suggestions for airport management? a. airport and airlines should apply more advanced security equipment and devices. b. execute more trainings to ground staff C. have a better cooperation with police and fire departments d. increase public security awareness of passengers and staff"},{"q":"What will China's aviation industry be like in 10 years from now?","a":"绿色环保，节能高效，经济实惠，安全放心... I'm pretty sure to say that in the future, the aviation industry in China will grow safer, moreconnectedtotheareasandcheapertocustomersaswell. Have you ever encountered hijacking? What should you do if you encounter a hijack? Pilot: make sure the cockpit door is closed and focus on the control, monitor cabin situation thru cockpit door surveillance camera, try not to irritate the hijacker and negotiate with them ATC: report my situation to ATC squawk 7500 and request landing priority Airport: request police, armed intervention, anti-terrorist force, fire and medical services on arrival"},{"q":"How to prevent hijack/terrorism from happening?","a":"First, we can improve airport security check to prevent terrorists and dangerous goods from entering the airport and aircraft. Second, both pilots and cabin crew can improve their skills to fight against the terrorism or hijack."}]},{"title":"航食CATERING","qa":[{"q":"Do all flight crew members have the same food on board?","a":"No, the captain and F/O cannot eat the same food simultaneously (at the same time). Because pilot might be food poisoned and become incapacitated, it will endanger flight safety. 2. Is it necessary to security check the catering staff? Yes, according to CAAC regulations, any person boarding the aircraft must be security checked to ensure safety."}]},{"title":"健康问题HEALTHISSUES","qa":[{"q":"Is it necessary for pilots to keep healthy?","a":"Do you think it is very important for pilot to stay healthy? Yes, it is necessary for pilots to keep healthy, pilot usually work in pressurized altitude and sometimes we work under high workload/pressure, we need to keep our physical conditions. 2. If you feel uncomfortable during the flight, what would you do? a. advise FO/captain and let him take control b. seek doctors on board, if necessary conduct first aid if necessary request priority landing, request airport medical assistance. Have myself substituted/replaced the next flight."},{"q":"If one pilot become incapacitated, what will you do?","a":"use standard callout, takeover the control, call cabin crew for help, make a PA find a doctor, declare distress, carry out first aid, contact ATC, request priority landing, medical assistanceonarrival"},{"q":"What may cause crew's incapacitation?","a":"What are the possible reasons for crew incapacitation? There aremany reasons for incapacitation a. health problems like not enough rest and high workload, heavy physical and mental stress b. depressurization C. diseases like hyperventilation,hypoxia,heart attack d. foodpoisonous serious illness and injuries 5. How to avoid crew's incapacitation? How to minimize the risks of incidents caused by crew's incapacitation? a. pilot should have enoughrestand eat healthily b. keepagoodexercisinghabit C. do not eat the same food on board when flying, healthy food diet d. always follow SOP and maintain situation awareness when depressurization happens e. regularphysicalexamination"}]},{"title":"复杂机场、高原机场和滨海机场COMPLICATED","qa":[{"q":"What is high elevation airport?","a":"What is coastal airport? High elevation airport means an airport's elevation exceeding 1400m Coastal airport is the airport beside or adjacent to sea and ocean."},{"q":"Before landing at high elevation airport , what should flight crew be careful","a":"about/what preparations should a pilot make? Terrain - complicated terrain and potential of terrain crash Weather - adverse weathers such as wind shear, crosswind, turbulence, snow, icing, sand and dust, icing A/C control problems because of the air density, pilots have difficulty control a/c, ENG power downgrade/hard to go around hypoxia/incapacitation/poorradio signal a. Make terrain analysis and calculate MOCA (Minimum Obstruction Clearance Altitude) during departure and approach/be careful about high terrains b. Make weather analysis, it can be very complicated in high elevation airport C. Be aware that the a/c performance especially the ENG performance will be dramatically reduced due to low air density, a/c will respond slower to control inputs, and needs longer runway for landing d. hypoxia/ radio communication(terrain) 4. Comparing sea level airport with high elevation airport, will there be any difference in aircraft's performance? Yes, due to low air density in high altitude, a/c performance especially the ENG performance will dramatically reduce/decrease, a/c control(inputs) will respond slower than usual So aircraft makes a bigger turning curve and needs a longer runway to land, and needs elevation airport is above 3000m"},{"q":"What should you be careful about if you takeoff or landing at coastal airport?","a":"I should be careful about the weather because there are many complicated weathers like thunderstorm, wind shear and runway condition are always damp, wet due to constant rain at coastal area."},{"q":"Are there any special qualifications for a pilot to fly high elevation airports? And","a":"what are they? yes, a pilot need to a. beexperienced,accumulateenoughflyinghours b. acceptspecialtrainingongroundsimulator specialphysicalexamination d. agebelow55 doublecaptainonboard 运营人要求Foroperatorsitrequires 航空公司在一般高原机场连续运行两年且至少积累500个起落后，方可进入高高原机场。 原则上在高高原机场飞行的机长年龄不超过55 岁，除已经取得高高原机场资格的驾驶员外，副驾驶需要具备总计1200 小时或以上的飞行经历，包括本机型100小时或以上的飞行经历，方可进入高原机场运行 此外，高原机场飞行的飞机供氧能力应符合所运行高原机场及航路的应急下降和急救用的补充氧气要求，并满足机组人 员在着陆后至下一次起飞前的必要供氧要求，在高高原运行的飞机，座舱增压系统也需要改装 相关图片描述 高原飞行注意事项： (1)该高原机场的地形特点 (2)该高原机场的气象特点 (3)该高原机场的通讯导航特点 (4)在该高原机场运行时。飞机性能的衰减(加速、减速慢，转弯半径大) (5)该高原机场起飞、落地的标准 (6)该高原机场地面保障能力(加油、电源、气源等) 进行特殊机场理论学习，考试合格，运行经历时间满300小时后，由具备该航线飞行经历的人员带飞检查 后可执行这些特殊机场的飞行运行"},{"q":"What factors may cause a/c to overrun the runway after landing?","a":"for example, braking action (standing water, icing and snow on runway). skills (poor simulator training and situation awareness), gear up landing/belly landing, fiapless landing, unstable approach, tailwind so on"},{"q":"Have you ever experienced takeoff or landing on the short and narrow runways?","a":"No, I am flying DA-40, usually the runway is long enough for takeoff and landing"},{"q":"What kind of runway may be considered as short and narrow runway?","a":"Generally, if the runway is less than 1800m long and 45m/30m wide, then it's a short andnarrowrunway But different types of a/c require different runway length and width. (For example,B737A3201500-1800m,theA380 requiresa runwaylengthof3000m for takeoffand landing) 标高，常年气候温度，机场性质等也对定义短窄跑道有影响，如高原机场跑道长度均在 3000m以上"},{"q":"Are there any risks when pilots operate on short and narrow runway?","a":"Yes, when takeoff or landing on short and narrow runway, the a/c may overrun the runway and veer off the runway. A/C may suffer structural damages like landing gear damages And passengers and crewmay suffer injuries 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"初始训练、复训和模拟机训练 INITIAL TRAINING,","qa":[{"q":"What type of aircraft are you flying now? can you say something about your","a":"aircraft? training and cross country training) It's a single propeller aircraft. it can accommodate/carry 4 people glide performance, long endurance, light weight, long life span, short takeoff and landing distance, cheap 2. ( Can you describe your flying experience? How many flying hours have you flown? I have completed private pilot license and instrument rating license (commercial license) training. and accumulated xx flying hours"},{"q":"How did you get along with your flight instructor?What have you learned from","a":"your flight instructor? He is an experienced and skillful instructor, he always has patience on me, and he teaches me SOP, flying skills, aviation knowledge (discipline, awareness of flight, responsibility)andsoon"},{"q":"Describe your first solo-flight","a":"My first solo flight was in Yichang Airport/Zhanghe Airport, 2 months ago, I was very nervous when I entered the cockpit but I calmed down and had a good control of my a/c during my flight, at last I successfully made my first solo flight."},{"q":"What did you learn from your first solo flight?","a":"I have learned many things from my first solo flight. First, we should make good preflight preparation to make flight easier and safer. Second, we should follow the checklist andfollowSOPtoensureflightsafety."},{"q":"What have you learned from your flight instructors?","a":"I have learned many things from my flight instructor. First, I have learned many flight skills from my flight instructor. Second, I have learned how to be a responsible man"},{"q":"Where do you have your recurrent training?","a":"I am a student pilot but I know it is in my company's training center (it includes theory and simulator training for emergency procedures)"},{"q":"How often do you have your recurrent training? How many hours are you required","a":"to complete each time? I do recurrent training every 6 months, and it lasts 12 hours every time 9. Do you think it's necessary for pilots to have recurrent/simulator training? a. FamiliarizewithSOP b. Improve situation awareness and reduce human errors C. Improve flying skills We need recurrent training because we can review some emergency situations and emergency procedures such as hydraulic failure, engine failure, depressurization and so on to improve our flight skills."},{"q":"what training subjects do you review in your recurrent training?","a":"模拟机复训基本会把所有特情飞一遍，也可根据飞行员自身弱点重点训练 we will training all the normal procedures including traffic circuit, holding pattern, approachandsoon and training abnormal situations such takeoff abortion,engine failure and so on. 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"液压系统HYDRAULIC SYSTEM","qa":[{"q":"Is the hydraulic system important to flight operations? What is the function of","a":"hydraulic system? Yes, it provides hydraulic pressure to operate flight control surfaces such as rudder, elevator, aileron and other surfaces (a/c will lose control without it) and landing gear(extend and retract, braking, nose gear turning) wheel brakes nose wheel steering landinggearretraction/extension flaps and slats thrust reversers spoilers/speed brakes flight control surfaces cargodoors/loading ramps windshield wipers propeller pitch control 2. How many hydraulic systems are there on your aircraft? What are the functions of hydraulic system? I fly DA-40, there is only one hydraulic system to control the brakes.,but I know for B737 andA320have3hydraulicsystems b. B737A,B,Standby C. A320Blue,green,yellow"},{"q":"When one hydraulic system fails, will it affect the flight safety? /What are the","a":"consequences of the hydraulic system failure in flight? DA-40 only has one hydraulic system, when it fails, the a/c will have problem to brake during landing, and may cause veer off/overrun the runway, structural damages to gears and injuries. for big aircraft, hydraulic system is designed on a redundant consideration, if one hydraulic system failed, the other two systems will still cover and provide pressure, and the hydraulic pumps can be driven by many means of sources. if all hydraulic systems failed, they may not able to extend the flaps, steer the nose wheel, and even brake the aircraft, it will be very dangerous. Hydraulic pumps can be driven by varieties of sources Manual. In many light aircraft, a manual hydraulic pump provides pressure for wheel brakes or flap extension and retraction. Engine Driven. Pumps are frequently mounted on the engine accessory gear box. Electric. Both AC and DC motors are utilized to power hydraulic pumps with three phase AC motors being most common. Pneumatic. Bleed air powered motors are utilized on some aircraft to drive hydraulic pumps. Hydraulic. A Power Transfer Unit (PTU) allows the hydraulic pressure of one hydraulic system to drive a pump to pressurize a second hydraulic system without any transfer of hydraulic fluid. Depending upon the installation, a PTU can be single or bi-directional. Ram Air Turbine. In the event of an emergency, some aircraft have a Ram Air Turbine (RAT) that can be extended into airstream to generate hydraulic pressure. Consequencesofhydraulicpressure loss Depending on the specific failure or the extent of damage to the hydraulic system(s), the following effects could result: Loss ofcontrol Partial orcomplete lossof control overspecific controlsurfaces Lossofautopilot Reversionto adegraded flight control law Impact on/collateral damage to other systems (e.g. due to a ruptured hydraulic pipe) PossiblelossofETOPSand/orRVSMcapability Loss of low visibility landing capability due to degraded autopilot or flight controls Difficulties with normal landing gear extension Inability to retract landing gear Inability to extend/retract high lift devices such as flaps or slats Reduced braking capability upon landing Lossofanti-skidsystems Inability to actuate thrust reversers Lossofnosewheel steering"},{"q":"When there's a hydraulic fluid leakage/leaking, what will you do?","a":"If you encounter hydraulic system malfunction, what will you do? As far as I know, when there's a hydraulic fluid leakage, On ground: report and let the maintenance staff to deal with it In flight: follow SOP, perform checklist (check fluid quantity, find out reasons), report ATC, in case of total loss of hydraulic pressure, request diversion, request longest runway airport assistance. 航线飞行员答案(机长答案) If my hydraulic system failed, I will focus on the control to maintain my aircraft for a stable flight, check which part of the flight control is affected, monitor closely on my aircraft airspeed, altitude and attitude. Try to fix the problem If the situation is worse, I will report to ATC and request diversion to the nearest suitable airport, prepare for possible evacuation and inform pax and crew to be ready. Also request to land on a longer runway and airport maintenance and medical services to be ready on arrival. 5. If your landing gear cannot be extended on approach, what will you do? a. Abort approach and join the holding pattern b. Follow SOP and perform the checklist C. Try to re-extend the landing gear manually/manual extension or gravity extension d. Make a low pass for visual check to confirm that the landing gear is completely down e. If all failed: dump/burn fuel and prepare for belly landing, make a PA for impact and prepare for evac after landing."},{"q":"If you decide to make a belly landing, what preparations should you make before","a":"landing? If I decide to make a belly landing, I will: A/C:check the FOB/fuel, dump/burn fuel b. ATC: declare emergency and report to ATC C. AIRPORT: request assistance such as foam the runway, fire, medical services d. PASSENGERS&CREWS: make a PA to inform them, get ready for belly landing (brace for impact/fasten seat belts) and emergency evacuation on landing."},{"q":"Do you think belly landing is safe? Why?","a":"What consequences might belly landing bring to aircraft? No, I don't think belly landing is safe, it can cause a/c structural damage, fire and explosion,passengersandcrewsfatalitiesandcasualties Yes, sometimes, belly landing is still one safe way to land the a/c when we encounter major control problems, if the pilot maneuvers the a/c well it won't cause injuries and it's saferthanotheroptions. 8. In what situation that will cause belly landing? a. Hydraulic malfunction and cause gear extension failure b. pilot makes human errors during landing and forget to extend the gear C. gearindicationproblem 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"空调系统AIR CONDITIONING SYSTEM","qa":[{"q":"What are the functions of air conditioning system?","a":"Air conditioning system is used to provide cool air and warm air to adjust the temperatureandmake persons on board safeand comfortable pressurizeair,providehumidity"},{"q":"If an air conditioning system fails, will it affect the flight safety?","a":"if it fails, the cabin and cockpit will lose temperature control (too hot or too cold).the pressurization system may malfunction, the aircraft will encounter flight safety problems."},{"q":"If an air conditioning system fails during flight, what will you do?","a":"I fly DA-40,it doesn't have air-conditioning system,(usually we open side window for ventilation and use cockpit heating function to bring hot bleed air from engine) but I know A320andB737have2ACpacks/systems. I will put on oxygen mask, execute emergency descent, follow sOp, perform checklist/QRH, start APU (ventilation and power generation), report to ATC, and request diversionandairportassistance"}]},{"title":"电力系统ELECTRICALSYSTEM","qa":[{"q":"What are the functions of electrical systems?","a":"Electrical system can provide electrical power to aircraft: lights, navigation, instrument indications An electrical system is an integral and essential component of all but the most simplistic of commercial jet aircraft. However, the electrical system for aircraft at both ends of the complexity spectrumsharemanyof thesamebasic components. All aircraft electrical systems have components with the ability to generate electricity. Depending upon the aircraft, generators or alternators are used to produce electricity. These are usually engine driven but may also be powered by an APU, a hydraulic motor or a Ram Air Turbine (RAT). Generator output is normally 115-120V/400HZ AC, 28V DC or 14V DC. Power from the generator may be used without modification or it may be routed through transformers, rectifiers or inverters to change the voltage or type of current."},{"q":"What may cause electrical failure?","a":"Therearemany reasons for electrical failure: lightning strike, electrical fire and smoke, short circuit, engine and generator failure and so on,impropermaintenance"},{"q":"If you encounter electrical system failure, what should you do?","a":"I fly DA-40, if I encountered electrical system failure, I will lose instrument indication/display (for PFD and MFD) and have use traditional instruments. I can use backup battery/backup power (or use traditional instrument) and request diversion/returning/landing as soon as possible, because it only maintains/lasts for about 30 mins"},{"q":"What kind of problems will be caused by electrical failure?","a":"Electricalfailurecancauselotsofproblems: lighting problems, flight control problems, electronic instrument indication problem.. communication and navigation problems,electrical fireand smoke Dangerous of electrical failure Loss of some orallof primary power generation capability Loss of all components and systems powered by thefailed bus Lossofan individual component Potential loss of aircraft should the fire become uncontrolable, loss of busses; systems or components due to the fire or as a result of electrical isolation procedures; smokeand/orfumes Defensesofelectrical failure Multiple primary generators and, where applicable, secondary (APU) or tertiary (RAT) generator installation. Multiple layers of redundancy greatly reduce the potential for loss of all electrical generation capability. Components connected to the bus have individual circuit protection which, in the event of a component failure protect the bus from overload and thus protect the remaining components. A bus failure is more typically the result of a failure of the power source supplying the bus and not the failure of the bus itself. As an example, the failure of a TRU could result in the loss of the DC bus that it powers. Depending upon the system design, provisions for an alternate power source may allow the bus to be restored. Circuit breakers (CB) exist to protect the system from overload in the event of a component failure and to prevent a potential fire from developing in the component itself by interrupting the electrical supply. In the event a circuit breaker \"pops\" in flight, the crew should comply with manufacturer and company policy when deciding whether or not the CB should be reset. Should a reset CB pop a second time, further reset should NOT be attempted. Note that some CB's such as those associated with fuel pumps should never be reset in flight. In the event of smoke, fumes or fire from a suspected electrical source, QRH procedures should be applied immediately while concurrently initiating electrical isolation procedure should be followed. Smoke and fume elimination procedures may become a necessity. Land AsAP."}]},{"title":"应急设备EMERGENCYEQUIPMENT","qa":[{"q":"What kind of emergency equipment do you have in the flight deck?","a":"I flyDA-40 we have safety axe, fire extinguisher cylinder,reflective vest oxygen mask, oxygen cylinder, smoke goggles, safety axe, first aid kit, ernergency flashlight, fire extinguisher, life vest, escape rope, ELT..."},{"q":"Why are pilots required to be familiar with emergency equipment?","a":"in case of emergency, for the safety of crew and passengers, and to protect the a/c, a pilot must be familiar with the emergency equipment, so he can respond from the emergency, save lives and reduce damages to a/c according to SOP and regulations, a pilot must be familiar with emergency equipment so he can have a quicker response in emergency situations, it will save passengers, crew and reducedamages to aircraft"},{"q":"How many oxygen supplies does your aircraft have?","a":"I fly DA-40 and it's an underpressurized aircraft(非气密式飞机) and flies below 3000m so it doesn't need oxygen supply system. But I know for big aircraft they have 3 oxygen supply systems: 现代民航飞机上的氧气系统有三类：驾驶舱失压或有烟雾时机组应急系统.客舱失压应急系统.手提式医疗供氧设备。 机组应急设备的氧气源为高压氧气瓶,正常压力1800psi.客舱氧气系统的氧气源是位于每组座位上方的旅客氧气发生器,每组座位一个.可持续供氧 10-12分钟.一旦触发不可关断.发生器和储氧袋杯形面罩一起构成了旅客服务组件.为了保险起见.面罩数都多于飞机上的座位数.当客舱失压时.氧气 面罩回自动放出.旅客只需抓住面罩，用力下拉即可触发发生器即可.飞机客舱处也存放有移动氧气瓶。 4. In which circumstances/under what situations do we use oxygen masks in flight? a. 座舱释压时应急供氧Oxygensupplyduringdepressurization b. 座舱灭火或有烟雾时保护性供氧smokeandfiresituations C. d. 高原机场运行 highelevation airportoperation(inordertopreventanyrisks of incapacitationwhenoperating) e. 系统失效如空调系统失效systemfailurelikeACsystemfailure In my opinion, there are many circumstances/situations that we need to use oxygen masks in flight, for example: depressurization situation, fire and smoke situations, medical emergency and first-aid to sick passengers, high elevation airport operations, air-conditioning system failure and soon. 5,When does oxygenmask dropduringflight? DA40doesn'thaveoxygensystembutIknow theoxygenmask can dropmanually by captain or automatically by system if smoke and depressurization is detected by system, the oxygen mask will drop automatically."}]},{"title":"皮托管PITOTTUBE","qa":[{"q":"How does pitot tube work and what is the function of pitot tube? What is the Pitot","a":"tube used for? Function: Pitot tube is used to measure the total pressure when aircraft flies. By measuring the dynamic and static pressure the pitot tube can help indicate ASl, VSl and altimeter. Pitot tube has a small opening at the front and it allows total pressure to enter the pressurechamber. 数据 皮托管阻塞 静压孔阻塞 指示空速 错误(×) 错误（×) 气压高度 正确 (V) 错误(×) 正确 (V) 垂直速度 错误(×) FPV 正确 (V) 错误(×) AP/FD 错误 (×) 错误 (×) 正确 (V) ATC高度报告 错误（云课堂 Static port 静压孔 is used to measure the static pressure in the air and calculate out the altimeter, vertical speed and so on."},{"q":"What is the indication when pitot tube is blocked?","a":"The aircraft data may be unreliable and inaccurate, for example, if the pitot tube is blocked by ice, the airspeed indication will be inaccurate and incorrect, unreliable."},{"q":"Whatwill youdoifpitottubeisblocked?","a":"We can followQRHor ECAM action to troubleshoot this problem. during flight, if it is blocked by ice, we should level change to avoid icing area and turn onpitotheatingfunction before flight, if it is blocked by ice, we should make walk around inspection and remove the ice and de-ice (spray de-ice fluid)"}]},{"title":"刹车系统BRAKESYSTEM","qa":[{"q":"Can you introduce the aircraft brake system?","a":"I fly DA4o and the hydraulic pressure controls the main gear brake,pedals and parking brake"},{"q":"What can you do if you lose your brake?","a":"pull control stickandapplyelevator I fly DA40, If I lose brakes, during landing I will shut down engine and let aircraft gradually stop. At the same time, I willfocus on align my aircraft nose along to the centerline, use minimum approach speed and request thelongest runway to land.(大机型加入反推，扰 流板，停稳撤离程序)"}]},{"title":"自动系统AUTOMATION SYSTEM","qa":[{"q":"Can you name some flight automation systems in flight?","a":"auto pilot, auto brake, auto throttle, auto landing, auto trim, auto fire warning TCAS, GPWS and so on DA4o has no flight automation system, we manually control the aircraft during the flight"},{"q":"In your opinion are these systems reliable?","a":"What are the advantages and disadvantages of automation system? They are very reliable, and they have backup systems. it will reduce pilot workload, reduce human error and improve flight efficiency/quality I don't think automation systems are always reliable. because electrical system might malfunction."},{"q":"Do you think pilot too much relies on automation system?","a":"yes, pilot should improve their own flying sklls in case of system malfunctions, we should trust ourselves instead of machine No, automation systems greatly reduce pilot workload, pilot still have the main control, and will have more time monitor system and focus on flight quality and safety"}]},{"title":"起落架和放轮程序 LANDING GEAR AND","qa":[{"q":"What are the functions of landing gear?","a":"it supports the aircraft to taxi and roll on taxiway and runway. and control the aircraft steering 同时还有减震，提升舒适度的功能absorbanderasethebumps"},{"q":"What will you do if the landing gear is not extended?","a":"I fly DA-40, the landing gear is just non-retractable and we will never worry about such question. I fly DA-42, we will make checklist before landing and always make sure the gear is down before landing. if it is still not extended I will make a manual extension, just pull the manual lever and make a pitch up maneuver in order to jar the wheel down by gravity. For civil jets, they have similar gear manual extension just like DA-42."},{"q":"How do you determine whether the landing gear is down and locked if the","a":"indication lights are unserviceable? If the gear indication is failed, we can make low pass to let ground personnel to check it for us for my aircraft DA40 it is unretractable landing gear"},{"q":"What are the procedures of manual/alternate extension?","a":"如图737飞机有3个手动放起落架的手柄，手柄直接控制上位锁，拉手柄则解锁，然后通 过重力将机轮甩出。 a. Firstly. the pilot should confirm that gear position is up and cannot be extended. b. then pull up the manual extension lever for each gear to unlock/release the gear. C. finally use a pull-up maneuver to jar the gear down by gravity."}]},{"title":"军事活动MILITARYOPERATION","qa":[{"q":"Do you think the military has occupied much airspace?","a":"No, I don't think so, military airspaces are usually restricted in certain area and used for military training and national safety, we should improve airspace efficiency Yes, military area training is one main reason of delay and airspace congestion, their airspaceshouldbereduced How can the military and civil operations agree on future airspace a. improve navigation equipment, apply new navigation system b. improveATCworkefficiency C. optimize airspace structure and strengthen civil and military cooperation d. Military should choose an airspace that has less aircraft activities"}]},{"title":"拥堵空域AIRSPACE CONGESTION","qa":[{"q":"Why is it dangerous to fly in a busy airspace?","a":"workload (control inputs, SOP, checklist, visual check, monitor frequency/instrument) AIRPORT: many departing and arriving aircraft overhead the airport and the airspace is crowded- near misses and mid-air collision ATC: ATC always have heavy workload, and may cause some human errors WEATHER: bird, fog happens in the congested airspace, there will be more risky and dangerous."},{"q":"What measures can be adopted to prevent aircraft conflict from happening at busy","a":"airports? What should we do to prevent near misses? PILOT: maintain own separation, timely position reports, visual scan, TCAS. follow instructions strictly and readback correctly, good communication with ATC, use standard RT. when unsureconfim with Ac,monitorfrequency precisely,befamiliar with airportand procedures, type of approach,simulator training (flying skill and situation awareness) AIRPORT/ATC: increase traffic separation, perform flow control if necessary. higher efficiencyonseparation"}]},{"title":"无线电通讯RADIOCOMMUNICATION","qa":[{"q":"What are the methods to communication with ATc?","a":"For most of the time we use verbal/oral/voice communication in VHF and HF. In oceanic route we can also use data link (CPDLC) and SELCAL to communicate."},{"q":"Why is verbal communication being especially essential in aviation safety?","a":"Because voice communication can transmit pilots intention and ATC's instruction clearly. straightly and rapidly"},{"q":"If ATC uses non-standard phraseology in communicating with you, how do you","a":"respond? First, I should use standard word/radio communication/sky talk/Radiotelephony to make response, listen carefully and readback correctly, when unsure of the instructions, 1 must confirm with ATC. When mishear the instruction, I will let him say again."},{"q":"Sometimes non-standard phraseologies may bring threat to flight safety, why?","a":"Non-standard phraseologies may cause misunderstandings and pilot may execute a mishearing instruction/make a wrong decision."},{"q":"During flight operation, which crew member is responsible for RT communication","a":"with ATC? According to sOP, a. During ground operation, the FO communicates with ATC b. During flight, the PM/PNF communicates with ATC"},{"q":"When do you need to carry out radio check?","a":"How do you carry out radio check? If the radio quality is low,we will do/execute/carry out radio check in initial contactwith ATC on the ground, to adjust radio quality, by saying\" how do you read me?\" the readability ranges from1to 5,if theradio quality is perfect then the readability is5"},{"q":"Do you think the communication among crew members is important?","a":"Yes, a good communication will improve cooperation among crew and also improve SOPand CRM, it will ensureour safety."},{"q":"What factors might affect the effective RT communication between ATC and pilots?","a":"What may cause misunderstanding when communicating with ATc? There are many factors affecting RT communication, for example, a. condition of radio equipment(transmitter/receiver) b. terrain/mountains- poor signal quality C. badweathers d. Non-standard RT between pilot and ATC frequency jam from other pilot, loud background noise"},{"q":"In your opinion, what could pilots do to improve the quality of RT?","a":"Are there any efficient ways for the pilot and the ATC to avoid misunderstanding with each other? As far as I know, a pilot should a. radio check to avoid low readability b. use standard RT, improve pronuncjation, follow instructions strictly and read back correctly (speak clearly). Confirm or request say again if the clearance is unclear C. improve English and speak loudly and clearly."},{"q":"Are there any suggestions to ATc from a pilot point of view? What are they?","a":"First, the controller should use standard words to avoid misunderstanding. Second, the controller should keep good communication with pilots to ensure flight safety. Third, if the aircraft is in emergency situations, the controllers should offer emergency services to the aircraft."},{"q":"What are some communication requirements during an emergency?","a":"During an emergency the pilot should a. Transmit PANPAN when encountering urgency situation like sick passengers, fuel emergencyand so on b.. Transmit MAYDAY when encountering distress situation like fire and smoke, ENG failure andso on TellATC emergency situations and intentions clearly d. otherpilots in the frequency should stoptransmitting"},{"q":"Have you ever attended any English radio-telephony courses in your flight school?","a":"What did you learn? Yes, I learned the basic aviation English and RT in my aviation school. I learned the pronunciations of A to Z, standard words and phrases, and communication procedures.(And IknowRT isa radio English with fixed pattern)"},{"q":"Do you think it's necessary to have such kind of training? Why?","a":"Yes, it's very necessary it helps us communicate/talk with ATC and pilots effectively, and we can understand ATC transmissions/instructions/clearance, it's very basic but important to our actual/real flight."},{"q":"What's the difference between RT and everyday English?","a":"In my opinion, RT has fixed pattern and is clearer, simpler, it's a standard language used in aviation, we use RT to talk with ATC and other pilots: For example, we don't say ABC, we say Alpha, Bravo and Charlie, and three is tree, nine is nin-er. we also use standard words and phrases like Affirm and Negative to express YES and NO."},{"q":"What are your company's procedures to deal with radio communication/two-way","a":"radio failure in flight? If you encounter radio failure, what would you do? First, we will squawk 7600 to indicate we have communication failure. Second, we should follow the last instruction given by ATC and follow flight plan until to the destination. Third, we will follow the SOP, recycle the radios and re-establish communication with ATC. If possible, we may switch to emergency frequency 121.5. 手册答案 A. stay calm and track the source COMM failure B. mark and identify aircraft current position, execute corresponding SOP and checklists as per the training base's regulation and manual. C. Communicationfailure procedures a. squawk7600 b, perform blind transmission and position report trytocontactATCvia mobilephone d. have a good look outside, visual check the sky carefully for any potential traffic conflicts D.proceed flightas perthe latest/lastATC instruction and stick to the flightplan filed,or continue flight according the usual methods that's taken.(Consider to land as soon as practical but notas soon as possible based on aircraft type and airport conditions)"},{"q":"What would you do if you encountered receiver/transmitter failure?","a":"If we encounter transmitter failure,we squawk 7600 and squawk ident to confirm ATC instructions,andfollow flightplanned routeto destination If we encounter receiver failure, we will squawk 7600 and transmit blind, ask other aircraft to relay our messages to ATC,follow flight planned route to destination"},{"q":"When an air traffic controller has a strong accent, what might be the risk?","a":"Pilots may mishear the instructions and cause flight safety problems. Pilots may misunderstand the instructions or clearance from ATC (for example)and causeflightsafetyproblems And pilot needs time to confirm with ATC, it will jam the frequency, and aircraft in an emergency will miss his best timing to fly."}]},{"title":"燃油 FUEL","qa":[{"q":"Are you familiar with your company fuel policy? Can you say something about it?","a":"I am still a student pilot, and I am not familiar with them But I know company encourages us to save fuel, and we have bonus for saved fuel. And fuel quantities are calculated according to flight plan, weather, weight and balance, possible diversion and holding and so on."},{"q":"What are the factors that may influence fuel economy/fuel consumption?","a":"weather conditions, airspeed setting, altitude selection, aircraft weight, piloting skill. wind direction and so on"},{"q":"Do you think flying higher level can save more fuel?","a":"Do you think a high cruising altitude helps reduce fuel consumption? generally, /normally speaking.yes a. air density at high level is lower, and the drag is less, so it saves fuel b. but fuel consumption is affected by many factors such as aircraft limitation, weight and so on, we should choose our optimum cruising level. 1万米以上空气过于稀薄，对于发动机推力不足的飞机，会导致发动机推力减弱，升力降低，反而更耗油。 螺旋桨发动机限制在5000米"},{"q":"Do you think that pilots play an important role in saving fuel?","a":"How do pilots save fuel? What can pilots do to save more fuel? In my opinion, pilot plays an important role in fuel saving, pilot can save lots of fuel by selecting the optimum/best level, speed and route. b. approach. and request exit the holding to reduce delay. C. skillful pilots can maneuver aircraft well, avoid unnecessary maneuver and save more fuel 开放性话题 In my opinion, it's good, because it can cut/reduce company cost, and save/protect environment. For pilots, it can increase their income (But safety is the priority in flight, to save more fuel, pilots may not be willing to go"},{"q":"Can you say something about fuel dumping procedure?","a":"I fly DA-40, we don't have fuel dumping function, and it is not necessary for DA-40 to burn fuel, so there's no fuel dumping procedures. (But I know for bigger aircraft, they will enter the fuel dumping area to dump fuel, or join the holding pattern to burn fuel)"},{"q":"When does a pilot need to execute fuel-dumping procedure?","a":"In my opinion, when an aircraft encounters an emergency and needs to return/divert, if the a/c weight is still above maximum landing weight/overweight, it needs to dump/jettison fuel during normal flight, if the aircraft refuels too much, it will also dump fuel during flight beforelanding. (for example, the a/c encounters landing gear failure and need to execute belly landing. pilot needs to check FOB, if the a/c is still above maximum landing weight, then it needs to dump fuel to reduce damages and injuries)"},{"q":"Why do we need to dump fuel before emergency landing?","a":"In emergency situations, we should dump fuel to reduce our aircraft weight to avoid heavy landing,especially during returning. and it will also reduce the possibility of fire and explosion during emergency landing. 9. Which area do you think fuel dumping is not allowed? a. Lake,river,forest,city(crowded residential area),low airspace b. Pilot must dumpfuel in the designated area 一般选择3000米以上高空、海洋、山区、荒野进行放油。航空煤油经过雾化后，污染较小，但是每年仍有上百方吨的 航油放出，雨天放油污染更大"},{"q":"During fuel dumping, what assistance can ATC provide?","a":"a. ATCkeep separationfor thea/cdumping fuelwith otheraircraft b. ATC gives a/c instruction/clearance/navigation/guidance/vectors to the designated area (fueldumpingroute)fordumping ATC can provide information along the route for the dumping fuel aircraft d. ATC can contact airport emergency services for the aircraft if it is needed JobofATC separation of the aircraft from other traffic providing diversion clearance or facilitating delaying tactics by providing vectors or a holdingclearance prioritization of the emergency aircraft for landing allowing for a long final if requested assigning a discrete radio frequency when possible advising the airport emergency services and all concerned parties in accordance with localprocedures/protocols providing any information that might be requested by the crew such as weather. type of approaches available, runway length and other pertinent aerodrome details 相关图片描述 11, How doyou refuel a/c before each flight? Before refueling.we calculatefuelquantity according toflight plan During refueling, the ground staff will refuel the a/c, pilots should monitor refueling. check fuel contamination and close the fuel cap After refueling, pilot should check and confirm fuel quantity. open EFiS and check fuel gauge (fuel indicator), reset fuel quantity on my instrument"},{"q":"What safety factors should a pilot consider during refueling?","a":"During refueling,we must make sure that a. No one is smoking in the cabin b. Phones (electronic devices/electrical systems)are turned off C. Fuel is not contaminated d. Fire extinguishers standby Connect ground wire, no refueling during thunderstorms 下列情况下不应该给航空器加(抽)油 1.主发动机未熄火、轮档未放好或螺旋桨未停止转动、航空器防撞灯未关闭 2.未经航空客户代表同意 3.机坪能见度小于50m 4.航空器在机库内 5.机坪上有雷暴 6.航空器在通电、充氧、充电、明火作业 7.机场上空有沙暴、大雨"},{"q":"After refueling, how could you know you have correct amount of fuel on board?","a":"DA-40, we will visual check the fuel cap, and turn on MFD and check the fuel quantity (on the fuel gauge, parameter)"},{"q":"If you encountered fuel leakage during refueling, what will you do?","a":"a. First we must stop refueling b. Then contact ATC for fire services (as precaution) C. Contact ground staff for inspection or maintenance d. keep away from fire, electronic devices, get fire extinguishers standby"},{"q":"What factors might be considered in calculating the fuel quantity for each flight?","a":"There are many factors, such as distance from departure and arrival airports (flight plan). weather, wind, cruising altitude, diversion airport and so on"},{"q":"Have you heard about any incident or accident due to fuel emergency?","a":"Yes, I heard a B767 from Canada airline, it ran out of fuel during cruising, the crew glided the aircraft safely and made an emergency landing at an abandoned airport."},{"q":"What's the importance of fuel check?","a":"By checking the fuel, we can a. during flight, calculate endurance b. during walk around inspection, find out fuel leak if there's any C. during refueling.check fuel contamination d. (According to regulations, pilots must check fuel every half an hour)"},{"q":"What are the legal requirements for aircraft fuel quantity?","a":"The FOB should be enough to land at the first destination (pilot/captain will decide fuel quantityaccordingtoflightplan) The reserved fuel should maintain a flying of at least 30 mins for daytime and 45 mins for night time The diversion fuel should support the aircraft until it reaches to the alternate"},{"q":"According to regulations, what does\" minimum fuel\" refer to?","a":"Minimum fuel is not considered as fuel emergency. it's an information for ATC that fuel emergency will be declared if any delay happened. According to regulations, if the fuel endurance at 3000ft over destination airport is less than 45 minutes, we will declare minimum fuel. VFRdaytime30nighttime45IFR45mins"},{"q":"How would you report your fuel minimum situation to ATc? If you have minimum","a":"fuel situation, what would you do? I will report/inform/advise minimum fuel (not fuel emergency) to inform ATC that we will start to burn the reserved fuel if we are delayed any further. And request landing clearance as soon as possible. \"Wuhan Approach, CCA101, our fuel endurance is less than 45 minutes, request priority landing at Wuhan Airport.\""},{"q":"Under what circumstances do pilots need to declare fuel emergency?","a":"a. Change of current clearance to destination causes the a/c to use the reserved fuel b. The a/c has less than 30 min of flying/endurance C. Serious fuel leaking d. Remaining fuel is sufficient until landing 最低油量是飞行员在遭遇到油量最低值时，应该报告给管制的信息，管制在了解之后应当立即采取应急措施。 一般最低值是昼间30分钟续航时间，夜间45分钟续航时间，高度为机场标高450米。速度为等待空速。 燃油紧急情况是飞行中遭遇重大漏油事故，或者燃油不足以坚持到目的地等因素，综合考虑后机长宣布的 Mayday电文。 前者是告知，而后者是特情，两种情况告知ATC后都要马上安排落地，后者享有特情飞机的优先落地权。"},{"q":"What might cause fuel shortage/low endurance on board?","a":"There are many reasons for fuel shortage, for example: Strong head wind, fuel leak, flying at low level for a long time, wrong speed and configuration, spend too much time on holding pattern, going around and so on, wrong calculation on fuel quantity"},{"q":"What are the reasons of fuel contamination?","a":"a. inadequatepreflight inspectionbypilot b. lackofmaintenance C. supply improperlyfiltered fuel d. water,sand and other debris"},{"q":"What might be the consequences of fuel contamination?","a":"Fuel contamination may block the fuel filter/pipe and cause fuel supply shortage or even cut off the fuel supply, resulting engine lose power and shutdown. Too much water in fuel will cause engine failure and vibration."}]},{"title":"酒精 ALCOHOL","qa":[{"q":"Can you drink alcohol before the flight?","a":"What is the regulation on drinking alcohol? By CCAR, Pilots are not allowed to drink alcohoi 8 hours before each flight, besides before boarding. pilots are required to execute alcohol test before boarding, alcohol in blood should be less than 0.04%. Any violation of alcohol policy will lead to a license disqualification. Resuits of alcohol test will be directly submitted to Authority."},{"q":"Can you introduce your company's alcohol control policy? Do you know anything","a":"about drinking regulations in your company? I haven't gone back to my company yet but I know some companies forbid pilots to drink within 24 hours before a flight. Also there is an alcohol test before boarding."},{"q":"Is it necessary to reduce pilot alcohol intake?","a":"Do you think it is necessary for pilots to limit alcohol intake? What is the danger of alcohol overtaking? Yes, alcohol affects pilot's physical condition(health) and long-term intake will cause many side effects to body function such as slow reaction, alcohol dependence and other mental/physicaldiseases. it increases risks of pilot's human error and flight safety problems. (Pilot may forget proceduresandmakemoremistakesduringoperation)"}]},{"title":"飞控检查FLIGHTCONTROLCHECK","qa":[{"q":"When do pilots need to do the flight control check?","a":"Pilots do the flight control check before departure, when the aircraft starts up, pilot will check flight control systems before or during taxi. As a student pilot, we will examinate the flight control surfaces during walk around/cockpit inspection and use our hand to move the surfaces, and we will also use control sticks to check the surfaces are functioning well. 2. What is the procedure of the flight control check in your company's sOP? a. Movecontrolstickleftorrighttocheckailerons. b. Move control stick forward and backward to check elevators. C. Step pedals to check rudder. d. At the same time, monitor instruments to see if flight control surfaces are normal or jammed."}]},{"title":"情景意识 SITUATION AWARENESS","qa":[{"q":"What is situational awareness?","a":"I think pilot should always know where the aircraft is, what is to do next, where is the next destination, these are parts of situation awareness. Put simply, situational awareness (SA) means appreciating all you need to know about what is going on when the full scope of your task - flying, controlling or maintaining an aircraft - is taken into account. More specifically and in the context of complex operational and phenomena. Can loss of situational awareness lead to accidents? And why? What are the consequences if a pilot has poor situation awareness? Ofcourse,if losing situation awareness,pilots maymakehuman errors andmakewrong decision,this may causeaccidents. Fora pilot,situational awareness means having a mental picture of the existing inter-relationship oflocation,flight conditions,configuration andenergystateofyour aircraft as well as any other factors that could be about to affect its safety such as proximate terrain, obstructions,airspace reservations and weather systems. The potential consequences of inadequate situational awareness include CFIT,loss of control,airspace infringement, loss of separation, or an encounter with wake vortex turbulence, severe air turbulence, heavy icing or unexpectedly strong head winds; For a controller, situational awareness means acquiring and maintaining a mental picture of the traffic situation being managed and maintaining an appreciation of the potential for unexpected progressions or changes in this scenario"},{"q":"Why is situational awareness important?","a":"What might happen if you lose situational awareness? it is very important, if losing situation awareness, pilots may make human errors and make wrong decision and wrong maneuver, this may cause accidents, such as CFIT, mid-air collision, runway incursion 4. Would you give some suggestions to pilots to improve their situational awareness? What can a pilot do to maintain a good situation awareness? I think simulator training is a good way to improve situation awareness, and have a good cooperation and communication with crew and ATC, flying hours accumulation, follow SOP and checklist all the time, have enough rest before flight, balance work and life. 情境意识差的三大因素：疲劳fatigue，压力workload，超负荷工作overtime Factors contributing to a poor situation awareness Interruptions and distractions; Use of the local language on a frequency used by pilots who are not familiar with it: Pilot or ATCO workload; Poor radio discipline."}]},{"title":"驾驶舱修仙SMOKINGIN COCKPIT","qa":[{"q":"Do you smoke in the cockpit?","a":"Why is smoking forbidden in the cockpit? Because smoking will lead to serious flight conditions, such as poor situation awareness, cockpit smoke situation,chocking and so on."},{"q":"What is your opinion on your company's ban smoke policy?","a":"According to CAAC and company regulations pilot is forbidden to smoke in cockpit and I agree with it. because smoke will cause distraction and human error, there will be flight safetyproblem. Pilot should focus more on flight control and flight quality."},{"q":"What are the consequences of smoke in the cockpit? What are the dangers of","a":"smoking during flight? Pilot: will be grounded due smoke (12 months-36 months) or even disqualified for flight. cockpit: instrument will be contaminated due smoke aircraft: cause cockpit smoke block eyesight, pilot is easier to make human errors. CCAR要求 According to CCAR regulation, smoking is strictly forbidden/prohibited in cockpit, any crew violating the regulation will be severely punished. (Including e-cigarettes) Punishment: a.12monthsflightsuspensionfor firstoffence b.36 months flight suspension for repeat offence"}]},{"title":"红眼航班 RED-EYE FLIGHT","qa":[{"q":"Have your ever flown red eye flights? what kinds of flights are red eye flights?","a":"Red-eye flight means a flight taking off at late night From 12 o'clock midnight until 6 o'clock in the morning"},{"q":"When preparing for this flight, what can a pilot do to reduce the stress? / What can","a":"you do to recover after a red eye flight? We should avoid overtime work and make sure enough sleep and rest before and after the flight."},{"q":"How would you comment a red eye flight?","a":"I think red eye flights have potential risks and may also cause physical damage to pilot's health condition, night flight and poor visibility will also result in some human errors. Pilot is always under high pressure when flying red eye flight."},{"q":"Why would you think red eye flight is a trend in civil aviation?","a":"Pilot:more flying hours and more salary Passengers: Due to cheaper flights and increasing number of flights, people are willing to choose flights atdeepnight to save cost. Company: there will be moreflights, increase profit. Airspace congestion at daytime willbe reduced"},{"q":"What are the differences between daytime flight and night time flight?","a":"daytime: CAVOK, safer and pilot can use VFR and IFR during flight, visual check landmarks fornavigation and obstructions foravoidance night time: visibility is almost O and pilot relies on instrument and lighting to fly. there are more risks due to low visibility."},{"q":"During night flight, what kind of runway equipment can help you land safely?","a":"runway centerline lighting, runway edge lighting, PAPl lights, approach lights and so on. Localizer, glide slope, marker beacon 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"不稳定进近UNSTABLEAPPROACH","qa":[{"q":"How does unstable approach influence flight safety?","a":"Unstable approaches are easily leading to a unstable landing condition and furtherly cause damages to airplane and injuries to passengers POB: injuries or even deaths Aircraft: structural damage - e.g., high speed landing, land too soon or too late will cause geardamage,runway excursion oroverrun"},{"q":"How to avoid unstable approach?","a":"There are many ways to avoid unstable approach Human: simulator training and accumulate more landing experience. Maintain proper speed, configuration, power setting, flight track and altitude when landing. Just go around if anything wrong happens Weather: avoid landing in adverse weather conditions a. Keep the pilot informed regarding runway assignment, type of approach and descent/speed restrictions.That willallowfor proper planning and execution of the approach. Stable approaches require predictability and planning. Avoid last minute changes and advise the pilot as early as possible when changes are anticipated. b. Ensure the runway assignment is appropriate for the wind. Wet or contaminated runways, combined with cross/tail winds are often associated with runway excursions. C. Issue accurate and timely information related to changing weather, wind and airport/runwayconditions. d. Apply appropriate speed control/ restrictions. Assigning unrealistic speeds (too fast or slow) may lead to unstable approaches. e. Give preference to precision approaches over non-precision approaches. Precision approaches have vertical guidance which assists the pilot in maintaining the proper descent profile, resulting in stable approaches. f. Avoid instructions that combine a descent clearance and a speed reduction. Many aircraft can't descend and slow down simultaneously. Comply with operational flight requirements related to capturing the glide slope from below. Vectoring for an approach that places an aircraft on the final approach course above the glide slope is a leading cause of unstable approaches. h.Avoid close-in, last second runway changes, even to a parallel runway. To comply with the company's operational procedures and requirements, the flight crew must have time to properly brief the approach and rmnissed approachproceduretotherunwaybe 5. During approach, if you notice more than 1 dot deviation from glide slop, what may be the reasons for that? There are many reasons for unstable glide slope, for example, wind shear, improper maneuver,navigation inaccuracy and so on Unstable Approach usually happens when the aircraft applies improper approach speed, configuration,attitude,thrust and course 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"进驾驶舱程序 COCKPIT ENTRY","qa":[{"q":"What are your company's cockpit entry rules?","a":"Do you know the regulation or rules of entering into cockpit? When the pilot wants to leave the cockpit, he must make a phone call to the purser to make sure there is no passenger around the cockpit door. after leaving cockpit, pilot need to shut the door, When returning cockpit, pilot has to make sure there is no passenger around"},{"q":"Who may be allowed to enter the cockpit?","a":"The flight crew and the designated/authorized cabin crew, usually purser for catering. Supervisor/representative from Authority, any other personnel granted permission from Captain"},{"q":"In flight, what are the procedures for crew member to enter the cockpit?","a":"during normal procedures, crew member will use interphone to talk with flight crew to get permission into cockpit, or knock the cockpit door,or use the cockpit door surveillance camera. During abnormal procedures like hijack and terrorism accidents,there will be secret signs for knocking the door, use the password or speaking in the interphone. Besides only authorized crew member can enter into cockpit."}]},{"title":"冬夏季运行 OPERATION IN WINTER AND","qa":[{"q":"When you operate in winter, what should you consider?","a":"we should consider icing conditions. If there is ice accumulation on wings, we should carry out de-ice, and check ice ingestion to engine and pitot tube We should also consider the low visibility condition, and pay attention to the company minimum limitation. Poor braking action"},{"q":"When you operate in summer, what kind of threat do you face?","a":"In summer, adverse weathers like thunderstorms, CB, lightning strike, wind shear and heavyrainare likelyto happen in summer. besides, forest fire and ash cloud may affect the route"}]},{"title":"等待程序HOLDING PROCEDURE","qa":[{"q":"If ATC request you to hold at a significant fix, what should you do?","a":"I will hold as requested and confirm with ATC the reason of holding and how long I should hold. If I was required to hold for a very long time, I will check my fuel endurance or request to exit the hold later. if I am not familiar with holding procedure I will request from ATC"},{"q":"When you request specific holding instructions from controller, what information","a":"do you need? We need a lot of information such as holding fix, altitude, speed, heading, inbound track. outboundtime,right hand or left-hand pattern and so on"}]},{"title":"复飞GOAROUND","qa":[{"q":"What is missed approach?","a":"During landing, if a pilot cannot continue landing due to visibility or other reasons, he must stop landing and pull up to climb to a certain altitude for another chance of landing, we call this process missed approach."},{"q":"Why is going around important?","a":"It can avoid aircraft landing in dangerous conditions and ensure the flight safety."},{"q":"Can you tell me the go around procedure?","a":"push throttle/max thrust, then reduce (takeoff)flaps, retract landing gear, pitch up to climb, follow runway heading. and follow the missed approach procedure to designated altitude and report ATC"},{"q":"Can you tell me the go around procedure in your airport?","a":"e.g.举例 forfurtherinstruction"},{"q":"In what situations must you go around?","a":"What may cause a pilot to go around? a. If any condition exceeds the limitation, we must go around b. ATC can also instruct or advise pilots to go around for example: wind condition exceedance, unstable approach, high speed approach, runway incursion, landing gear problem, system malfunction and so on"}]},{"title":"失速STALL","qa":[{"q":"Does your aircraft have stall warning? What is it like?","a":"Howdoyou recoverfrom stall? DA-40 has stall warning when the aircraft stalls it will sound a \"beep\"\" buzzer\" voice audio/sound/aural warning,lighting warning Stall recovery: I will pitch down, push throttle and maintain configuration to recover. \"Speed, speed\"-\"stall, stall\""},{"q":"Why is stall dangerous to flight?","a":"If a pilot cannot recover from stall, the aircraft will spin and fall, or even crash. stall is especially dangerous to low altitude flight and big aircraft."},{"q":"Do you know how to avoid stall? What can a pilot do to prevent a stall?","a":"We should avoid flying into adverse weather conditions, and monitor airspeed carefully, improve pilot flying skill and accept stall training in simulator."}]},{"title":"标准喊话 STANDARD CALLOUT","qa":[{"q":"What is \"standard callout\"? Could you explain it in detail?","a":"They are oral termsused during flight(覆盖关键阶段，关键检查项，正常和特殊情况) There are many standard call out, for example, a. During takeoff, PNF should call out \"V1\", \"rotate\" and so on. b. During control handover, we should call \"you have the control\",\" I have the control\" C, During DH, we should call\" continue\" or\" go around\" Why is standard callout important for flight? By standard callout, it can make sure that flight crew know what they are doing and remain situation awareness, improve task sharing and communication, help complete the checklist During final approach, what standard callouts should be made by PM? During final approach, PM should call out \"hundred above\"\"minimum\" \"continue\"\" go around\"...these arerequired bySOp. 4. What's the standard call out during flaps extension? a. PFcontrolstheaircraft PM call \"check speed, altitude, and flap position like flaps one, flaps two\" b."}]},{"title":"性能评估PERFORMANCEASSESSMENT","qa":[{"q":"Do you make takeoff/landing performance assessment for each flight? Is it","a":"necessary? 根据跑道状况、飞机性能资料、机场参数、气象要素等，计算在当时情况下航空器性能限制起飞重量、航空器性能限制 着陆重量、所需着陆距离等(通常将数据输入适配机型的EFB或性能软件完成，或结合相关公式计算。) No, (FOB, aircraft weight) in flight school, DA-40 is good to land on almost every airport, and we only takeoff and land in good weather and runway conditions. Yes, it very important for a safe takeoff and landing, we will consider runway condition and weather conditions and calculate the takeoff and landing performance"},{"q":"What factors should you consider when making the performance assessment?","a":"What factors will influence the landing performance? We will consider many factors such as takeoff/landing weight, runway conditions and runway length, weather conditions (wind, temperature), airport elevation, configuration, speed and so on"},{"q":"Will you takeoff or land on a runway when the braking action is poor?","a":"Yes, I fly DA-40 even the braking action is poor, it won't take much runway to stop the aircraft, but we will be careful about veering off runway when landing and keep the aircraft in a good landing attitude. We will check the aircraft performance, runway length and weather, if the assessment is ok we will takeoff or land; if the assessment is lower than minimum, we will abort takeoff or go around, and use another better runway."},{"q":"If you find a patch of water on the runway, what will you do?","a":"before departure I should inform airport to clean the water because it contaminates the runway and will change runway braking action data before landing,I will adjust my aircraft to get ready to land on water patches,keep my aircraft in a good attitude and expect a longer runway length to land.I should also prepare forpossiblegoaround"},{"q":"Would you please introduce the procedures of the landing performance","a":"assessment of the aircraft you flying now? When we receive ATis, we should start the landing performance assessment, we should consider a. Runway conditions,including the elevation of runway,the length and width of runway andbraking action b. Weather condition, including visibility, cloud, wind speed and direction C. Aircraft performance, including airspeed, landing weight, configuration"}]},{"title":"高度偏离LEVEL DEVIATION","qa":[{"q":"Could you please name a few of major factors that may cause altitude deviation?","a":"thereare lots of factors: a. turbulence, wind shear, down/up draft b. human error, improper maneuver, wrong altimeter/altitude setting 2. What could a pilot do to avoid altitude deviation? pilot should avoid flying into adverse weather, monitor weather radar, altitude, TCAS or GPwS all the time, more simulator training. improve flying skills, maintain situation awareness enhance cooperation and communication, to avoid level deviation caused by human errors"}]},{"title":"签派 DISPATCH","qa":[{"q":"Do you know dispatcher? What does dispatcher do?","a":"What kinds of support do you need from him/her during operation? 搜集飞行信息，制定飞行计划，协调飞机放行，保障和监控安全飞行，联系应急部门 in normal operation, dispatcher will collect flight information, make flight plan and monitor our flight in abnormal situation,dispatcher can help us contact with emergency departments. For example, diversion situation, dispatcher can communicate with ATC in alternate airport and arrange the parking gate and other assistance. When would you contact with your company dispatcher? a. before flight, dispatcher will meet us in the briefing room and handout flight plan b. during flight, if we encounter any abnormal situation, we should inform dispatcher"},{"q":"How would you contact with a dispatcher in the air?","a":"we will use the satellite phone/ACARS to contact with our company's dispatcher."}]},{"title":"飞行作风FLIGHTDISCIPLINE","qa":[{"q":"What is your opinion on flight discipline? How to develop a good flight discipline?","a":"I think flight discipline is very important for flight safety. Pilot should always follow SOP and remember the regulations, do not make any jokes and keep a good awareness during flight, be responsible to crew and passengers, CRM- cooperation and communication. (no smoking, no drinking, no extra talking..)"},{"q":"How can loss of flight discipline affect flight safety?","a":"if pilots have no flight discipline, they are likely to encounter human errors, lose situation awareness, violate/break regulations or even make wrong decisions, there will be flightsafetyproblems."}]},{"title":"机务手册/飞机维修记录本和飞行记录本","qa":[{"q":"What is Maintenance Logbook?","a":"it is a logbook that records all the aircraft malfunction and wrong indications discovered in the past, flight crew and maintenance staff should also record the problem if they find any during operation. Why do flight crew check it before flight? Flight crew can know what happened to the aircraft in the past, it helps pilot make the right decision, be more familiar with the aircraft."},{"q":"What items should the flight crew check on the maintenance logbook?","a":"Before flight, we should check logbook to see if there is any malfunction recorded by previous pilot. We will also check maintenance staff's reply to the problem and the remaining problem"},{"q":"When do flight crew check maintenance logbook?","a":"Before each flight, during pre-flight operation 飞机履历本(不可放在飞机上，机务持有，记录所有飞机维修记录)，飞行记录本(放在飞机上， 机组可以填写，机务检查)发动机记录本(不可放在飞机上，记录飞机发动机维修保养情况) 5. What is flight logbook? What is the function of flight logbook? flight logbook is a record of flight, it contains lots of training information such as flight training subjects, time of training, and signature of flight instructors and so on. Nowadays we use Cloud License to record these information飞行履历本 6. Who is responsible for flling flight logbook? In Company,the copilot is responsible for fillinglogbook,and the captain signs. In flight school, student pilots willfill the logbook and flight instructor willbe signing the logbook.PiCwillsignthelogbook"},{"q":"Is there any requirement on filling the flight logbook?","a":"We need to write down all information about flight, like departure and landing time, FOB, POB, cargo. If there is any aircraft malfunction during flight, we will record it to flight logbook."}]},{"title":"活塞和涡轮发动机 PISTON AND TURBINE","qa":[{"q":"Do you know the differences between turbine engine and piston engine?","a":"设计上 piston engine (also referred as blade engine) has a long history and now is normally assembled on general aircraft and small light aircraft, it is fuel efficient at low speed, it is a stable and reliable type of engine, but the maximum thrust/speed produced from the engine is limited due to its design, and it is heavier than turbine engine, so it is not suitable for high-speed flying and high-altitude cruising. endurance and produces higher power and lift to allow bigger aircraft to fly at a cruising level, it is lighter and generates stronger thrust, it has a good performance at high altitude flying 工作原理上 Piston engine includes in-line 直列式, opposed 对置式, V-type V 型,radial 径向式,the more pistons it has the larger space it would occupy in the engine, it takes four-stroke process to produce thrust, air intake, compression, powering and then exhaust, each process works individually. Turbine engine includes turbofan 涡扇(mostly used in airliners), turbojet 涡喷(mostly used in fighter jets), turboprop 涡桨(used in many different aircraft), turboshaft 涡轴, by producing thrust it will experience a continuous process, air intake by turbofan, ignition in chamber and then exhaust, each process works simultaneously."},{"q":"How doesthepiston enginework?","a":"It takes four-stroke cycle to produce thrust, air intake by the inlet valve to mix the air and fuel, compression by the piston ,ignition and powering by the spark plug firing(火花塞 点火)and thenexhaustthegas bythe outletvalveeachprocess works individually."},{"q":"If the detonation(爆震) of piston happened on aircraft what will you do?","a":"Detonations affect the life span of a piston engine, it could be resulted from an early ignition, air and fuel mixture problem, fuel contamination or engine overheat, so I will have it checkedbymechanic engineer."}]},{"title":"槽纹跑道GROOVED RUNWAY","qa":[{"q":"During flight preparation, how do you know you are in grooved runway condition?","a":"We can know runway conditions from ATC, NOTAM, ATIS, Pilot reports and visual check."},{"q":"Do you often check if the runway is grooved?","a":"Of course, we must be aware of the runway conditions that we are going to use before flight"},{"q":"If there is standing water, will it affect flight safety?","a":"yes, it will influence aircraft landing performance during landing: reduce the friction between tire and ground, landing water bounce, and cause possible overrun runway, veer off runway, there will be a possible hydroplaning if unproperly maneuvered."},{"q":"What should you consider if the runway is covered with patches of water?","a":"Runway will be wet and slippery, braking action is poor, I will consider my takeoff and landing distance, aircraft performance, aircraft weight, and make assessment before takeoff and landing. Avoid veering off or running out of runway."}]},{"title":"危险态度HAZARDOUSATTITUDES","qa":[{"q":"How can hazardous attitudes affect flight safety?","a":"it will affect situation awareness, cause human errors and endanger our flight safety"},{"q":"How can pilot avoid hazardous attitudes?","a":"We should strictly follow regulations, manuals and SOp, have good communication and cooperation with crews."}]}]},{"title":"五、缩略语 ABBREVIATIONS","subs":[{"title":"管制员和驾驶员数据链通讯CPDLC","qa":[{"q":"What is CPDLC? How do you use CPDLC?","a":"CPDLC stands for Controller and Pilot Data Link Communication We normally use CPDLC during international flight, we will receive ATC instruction through CPDLC and our DCDU will display the instruction, when reading the instruction, wewillpressWILCOtoconfirm In flight school, DA-4O doesn't have CPDLC, so we only use Chinese voice communication in flight training"},{"q":"Can CPDLC replace the voice communication in critical flight phases?","a":"No, I don't think it's possible, because pilot is usually under high workload in critical phases, they have to follow SOP, execute checklist, maneuver aircraft, monitor instruments and so on, they will not have time to read CPDLC test message. 3, If you have communication failure, how do you use CPDLC to contact with ATC? DA-40 doesn't have CPDLC,Idon'tknowmuch about it,but inmy flight school, if encounter communication failure, I will follow SOp and do checklist to examine frequency and equipment, squawk 7600 and transmit blind, try to fly to the destination asplanned"}]},{"title":"空中防撞系统 TCAS TratFic Alert and Collisjon Avaidahce sysep","qa":[{"q":"Do you have TCAS on your aircraft?","a":"I fly DA-40 NG, and yes Garmin 1000 has a simple version of TCAS called TIS(Traffic Information Service) it works the same just like TCAS and by transmitting transponder signal it senses traffic around the aircraft. When traffic is closing, the system will sound \"traffic\" to alert crew."},{"q":"Have you ever experienced TCAS alert during flight? If not, have you heard of","a":"any accidents regarding to TCAs? 联合航空和环球航空空难： No, I haven't but I know there was a mid- air collision happened in Germany in 2002, one aircraft followed TCAS to climb, another aircraft followed ATC's wrong instruction to climb as well instead of following \"descend\" alert from TCAS, so they collided with each other."},{"q":"What are the possible reasons for mid-air collision?","a":"Pilot human errors: notfollowing TCAS RA,poor situation awareness,poor communication with ATC,not monitorinstrumentsandsky Airport&ATC: Congested airspace,ATC heavyworkload,bad weather (low visibility)"},{"q":"What can we/pilots do to avoid mid-air collision?","a":"Aircraft: monitor instrument precisely and check radar, strictly follow TCAS alerts and advisory Pilot: visual scanning the sky if possible/keep a good look-out, follow SOP and have a good task sharing in flight. Communication: good communication to ATC, monitor frequency carefully-follow instruction strictly and read back correctly, use standard phraseology. when unsure of the instruction,confirm withATC Company: more simulator training, improve situation awareness and flying skills 相关图片描述"}]},{"title":"可控飞行撞地 CFIT Cortrolled Hight nto Terrain","qa":[{"q":"Why does CFIT sometimes happen?","a":"What kind of pilot's operation habits might lead to CFiT? Some pilots don't follow sOp strictly with poor situation awareness and have poor cooperation/communication with crew members, poor flying skill, not enough flying hours,poor monitoring and scanning Poor communication with ATC, not strictly follow ATC instruction and have poor monitoring on instrument, improper maneuver of aircraft, fly high rate of descent and high speed. poor visibility,highterrain,obstructed airspace Weather: Rain, turbulence, and icing, may increase the workload of the pilot and cause interference reducing the accuracy of radio navigation beacons. Poor visibility, particularly at night can contribute to disorientation and loss ofsituationalawareness. Approach Design and documentation: The depiction of an approach, and particularly step down fixes, on Terminal Approach Procedure (TAP) plates may not be clear. Approaches may take aircraft close to high terrain in order to comply with diplomatic or noise abatement constraints, or to deconflict with departure routes. Failure to use Standard Phraseology leading to confusion and misunderstanding. Pilot fatigue and disorientation. Approach and landing is a demanding phase of flight for pilots."},{"q":"How do we avoid CFIT in general?","a":"instruction to avoid, follow EGPWS advisory, good communication with crew and ATC, flying hours, flying skill, situation awareness. Aircraft: Aircraft should be equipped with EGPWS Weather: In case of bad weather pilot should divert or stay on the ground More widespread equipment of aircraft with TAWS. Greater awareness of Approach and Landing risks. Continuous Descent Final Approaches (CDFA). MSAW systems. Electronic Terrain and Obstacle Data (eTOD)"},{"q":"What measures does your company take to prevent aircraft fly-into-terrain?","a":"recurrent training-Simulator training, SOP and CRM training, cockpit briefing. task-sharing, checklist, QAR, CVR investigation"}]},{"title":"近地警告系统GPWS","qa":[{"q":"What is GPWS?What is the function of GPWS?","a":"GPWS is Ground Proximity Warning System, it is a system to alert pilots if the aircraft is in immediate danger of flying into the ground or an obstacle. (it is a system to helppilotavoid terrain andgroundcrash)"},{"q":"How does GPwS work?","a":"It depends on radio altimeter and flight computers to calculate distance information then display terrain to EFIS in different colors. It checks AOA, rate of descent and vertical distance, if any data exceeds limitation, GPWS will trigger warning It will give pilot sound(synthetic/aural) warning (\"caution terrain, terrain terrain, pull up pull up) and warning horn/buzzer, if the aircraft is close to terrain. Pilot must then followGPWS advisoryto pullup additionally: a. b. \"Don't sink\" for a significant altitude loss after TO or GA C. \"too low terrain/gear/flaps\" for unproper configuration with insufficient terrain clearance d. \"Glide slope\" for a possible undershooting whenever the aircraft descends below the glide slope from 1000ft. 3. What is radio altimeter? a. it will measure the actual vertical distance between aircraft and ground b. it consists of transmitter receiver, antenna and indicator C. it is mainly used during low altitude flight, especially during initial climb, approach and landing"},{"q":"What is EGPWS?","a":"EGPWS is Enhanced Ground Proximity Warning System It is an updated version of GPwS, integrating more functions to prevent collisions. 与 GPWS 相比,EGPWS 对全球地形数据、障碍物数据、机场数据等进行了勘测和录入，在一些 垂直高度变化大的地形(如断崖，峭壁)GPWS 不能及时报警的情况下(GPWS 主要依靠无线电高度 表，大气高度变化和垂直速度变化语预警)，EGPWS仍能提前提醒飞行员进行避让机动。但是， 飞行员需要在 EGPWS数据库覆盖的地区和 GPS 信号符合最低要求的地区飞行才能被 EGWPS 预 如单要掌摄飞机各系统是如何工作的，那么必须吃透《飞行机组操作手册》（FCOM）； 如果要掌握机组应该如何飞，那么必须吃透《标准操作程序》（SOP）：如果要掌握飞行操 作技巧，那么必须吃透《飞行机组训练手册》（FCTM）：如果要掌握飞机带哪些故项自还 可以飞，那么必须吃透《最低设备清单》（MEL）；如果要掌握在飞行中遇到故障时机组应 该如何处置，那么必须吃透《快速检查单》（QRH）；如果要掌握航班应该按什么政策。标 准、规定来飞，那么必须吃透公司《运行手册》如果要清楚自己的个性心理特征、清楚自己 的能力边界在哪里，那么请吃透《机组资源管理》（CRM）， Preare Chaptero TahieorContents Seefinno Tite Chapter Preface. Table of Content 737-600/700/800/900 Moclel fdentificarion Flight CrewTraining Manual 0.1 Introduetion Atbreviations Resision Record L.ist of Effecfive Pages Genetal Infomstion Ground Operations Takeoff and Initial Climb A330/A340 Clinh, C'muise, Descent ant Hokdog Appcoach and Missed Approach FLIGHTCREW Landng TECHNIQUESMANUAL Manenver Non-Nomal Operstion Indeg"}]},{"title":"重心CG","qa":[{"q":"What is CG? What factors may affect takeoff CG?","a":"CG is Center of Gravity a/c weight, fuel on board, number of passengers, cargo and baggage"},{"q":"What may happen when the cG is too far forward?","a":"It's hard to pull up the a/c during takeoff rolling and a/c nose may hit the ground when braking too much, potential nose gear damage when landing."},{"q":"How do you calculate CG?","a":"Pilots calculate CG according to the weight and balance regulation, a pilot need to make sure the aircraft has the correct arrangement of passenger seats, and correct amount of fuel according to the range of route, and correct weight of luggage/cargo loading. range of flight and refuel the right quantity of fuel)"},{"q":"How do you calculate CG?","a":"航校Before myflight,we will takeinto consideration ofzerofuel weight,aircraft weight, persons on board, and other weight and balance factors, then calculate them in"}]},{"title":"跑道视程RVR","qa":[{"q":"What is RVR? How can pilots obtain RVR before departure?","a":"RVR stands for Runway Visual Range Pilots get RVR from ATIS, ATC and dispatch."},{"q":"Does your company operate low visibility take-off (LVTO)?","a":"it depends on qualifications of crew, airport, airline regulation and aircraft equipment. Yes, when LVTO is effective, takeoff minimum is reduced to RVR 350m In my flight school, if the visibility is low, we will wait on the ground until it gets better"},{"q":"When will low visibility procedure be required?","a":"When RVR is below 400 meters, or when there are no runway centerline lights and RVR is below 500 meters And for example, the ILS system will be effective when the RVR is below 550m 4. Do you know Lvo? Yes, LVO stands for Low visibility Operation, airport will apply CAT Il and CAT Ill ILS approachandLVTOforasafeoperationundertherequirementof LVO"},{"q":"DoyouknowLVTO?","a":"It means takeoff procedure in lowvisibilitycondition,underthe operation of Lvo"},{"q":"What is PAPI?","a":"PAPImeansPrecisionApproachPathIndicator Normally we see 2 red lights and 2white lights during approach a. Morered lights-wearebelowtheGp (undershoot) b. Morewhite lights-weareabove the GP (overshoot)"}]},{"title":"水上延程运行ETOPS","qa":[]},{"title":"DME测距仪","qa":[]}]}];
